// Interview problems: worked examples, Young & Freedman University Physics 13th ed.. Fields: id, title, q (statement), figs, figDesc, sol (examiner-only reference), answers.
(window.EXAMPLES_BY=window.EXAMPLES_BY||{}).phys=[{"id":"2.1","title":"Cheetah\u0027s average and instantaneous velocity","q":"A cheetah is crouched 20 m to the east of an observer. At time t = 0 the cheetah begins to run due east toward an antelope that is 50 m to the east of the observer. During the first 2.0 s of the attack, the cheetah\u0027s coordinate x varies with time according to x = 20 m + (5.0 m/s²)t². (a) Find the cheetah\u0027s displacement between t₁ = 1.0 s and t₂ = 2.0 s. (b) Find its average velocity during that interval. (c) Find its instantaneous velocity at t₁ = 1.0 s by taking Δt = 0.1 s, then 0.01 s, then 0.001 s. (d) Derive an expression for the cheetah\u0027s instantaneous velocity as a function of time, and use it to find vₓ at t = 1.0 s and t = 2.0 s.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: use $\\Delta x = x_2 - x_1$, $v_{av}=\\Delta x/\\Delta t$, and $v_x = dx/dt$ as the limit of the average velocity.\n2. Positions: $x_1 = 20 + 5.0(1.0)^2 = 25$ m, $x_2 = 20 + 5.0(2.0)^2 = 40$ m, so $\\Delta x = 15$ m.\n3. Average velocity: $v_{av} = 15\\text{ m}/1.0\\text{ s} = 15$ m/s.\n4. Shrinking intervals from $t_1 = 1.0$ s: for $\\Delta t = 0.1$ s, $x(1.1) = 26.05$ m, $v_{av} = 1.05/0.1 = 10.5$ m/s; for 0.01 s it is 10.05 m/s; for 0.001 s it is 10.005 m/s. The values approach 10 m/s.\n5. Derivative: $v_x = dx/dt = (10\\text{ m/s}^2)\\,t$ (constant term drops out, $d(t^2)/dt = 2t$). At $t=1.0$ s, $v_x = 10$ m/s; at $t=2.0$ s, $v_x = 20$ m/s.\n6. Check: the cheetah speeds up from rest to 20 m/s, and the average over 1 s to 2 s (15 m/s) lies between 10 and 20 m/s.","answers":["(a) 15 m","(b) 15 m/s","(c) 10.5 m/s, 10.05 m/s, 10.005 m/s, approaching 10 m/s","(d) v_x = (10 m/s²)t; 10 m/s at 1.0 s and 20 m/s at 2.0 s"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 2.1"},{"id":"2.4","title":"Motorcyclist leaving town with constant acceleration","q":"A motorcyclist heading east through a small town accelerates at a constant 4.0 m/s² after he leaves the city limits. At time t = 0 he is 5.0 m east of the city-limits signpost, moving east at 15 m/s. (a) Find his position and velocity at t = 2.0 s. (b) Where is he when his velocity is 25 m/s?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: constant acceleration, so use the constant-acceleration equations. Take the signpost as origin and east as +x: $x_0 = 5.0$ m, $v_{0x} = 15$ m/s, $a_x = 4.0$ m/s².\n2. (a) Position: $x = x_0 + v_{0x}t + \\tfrac12 a_x t^2 = 5.0 + 15(2.0) + \\tfrac12(4.0)(2.0)^2 = 43$ m.\n3. Velocity: $v_x = v_{0x} + a_x t = 15 + 4.0(2.0) = 23$ m/s.\n4. (b) Time is not given, so use $v_x^2 = v_{0x}^2 + 2a_x(x - x_0)$, giving $x = x_0 + (v_x^2 - v_{0x}^2)/(2a_x)$.\n5. Numbers: $x = 5.0 + (625 - 225)/(2\\cdot4.0) = 5.0 + 50 = 55$ m.\n6. Check (long way): $v_x = 25$ m/s at $t = (25-15)/4.0 = 2.5$ s; then $x = 5.0 + 15(2.5) + \\tfrac12(4.0)(2.5)^2 = 55$ m.","answers":["(a) x = 43 m, v = 23 m/s","(b) x = 55 m (reached at t = 2.5 s)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 2.4"},{"id":"2.5","title":"Police motorcycle catching a speeding motorist","q":"A motorist traveling with a constant speed of 15 m/s (about 34 mi/h) passes a school-crossing corner, where the speed limit is 10 m/s (about 22 mi/h). Just as the motorist passes the school-crossing sign, a police officer on a motorcycle, who was stopped there, starts in pursuit with constant acceleration of 3.0 m/s². (a) How much time elapses before the officer passes the motorist? (b) What is the officer\u0027s speed at that time? (c) At that time, what distance has each vehicle traveled?","figs":["figures/ex-phys/ex2-5.webp"],"figDesc":"Sketch along an x-axis with origin O at the school-crossing sign. The officer starts at rest at the sign with constant x-acceleration 3.0 m/s²; the motorist moves with constant x-velocity 15 m/s. (No figure data beyond the problem statement.)","sol":"1. Approach: both move with constant acceleration (zero for the motorist). Take the sign as the origin and the direction of motion as +x; $x_0 = 0$ for both.\n2. Positions: motorist $x_M = v_{M0x}t$; officer $x_P = \\tfrac12 a_{Px}t^2$.\n3. (a) Set $x_P = x_M$: $v_{M0x}t = \\tfrac12 a_{Px}t^2$, so $t = 0$ or $t = 2v_{M0x}/a_{Px} = 2(15)/3.0 = 10$ s. The non-zero root is the passing time.\n4. (b) Officer\u0027s velocity: $v_{Px} = a_{Px}t = (3.0)(10) = 30$ m/s.\n5. (c) Distances: $x_M = 15(10) = 150$ m and $x_P = \\tfrac12(3.0)(10)^2 = 150$ m (equal, as required).\n6. Check: at the meeting point the officer\u0027s speed is twice the motorist\u0027s. This holds for any $a_{Px}$, because $x - x_0 = \\tfrac12(v_{0x}+v_x)t$ is the same for both while the motorist\u0027s average velocity is 15 m/s.","answers":["(a) 10 s","(b) 30 m/s","(c) 150 m each"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 2.5"},{"id":"2.7","title":"Ball thrown upward from a roof railing","q":"You throw a ball vertically upward from the roof of a tall building. The ball leaves your hand at a point even with the roof railing with an upward speed of 15.0 m/s; the ball is then in free fall. On its way back down, it just misses the railing. Find (a) the ball\u0027s position and velocity 1.00 s and 4.00 s after leaving your hand; (b) the ball\u0027s velocity when it is 5.00 m above the railing; (c) the maximum height reached; (d) the ball\u0027s acceleration when it is at its maximum height. Use g = 9.80 m/s².","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: free fall means constant $a_y = -g = -9.80$ m/s². Origin at the hand, upward positive: $y_0 = 0$, $v_{0y} = 15.0$ m/s.\n2. (a) $y = 15.0t - 4.90t^2$ and $v_y = 15.0 - 9.80t$. At $t = 1.00$ s: $y = +10.1$ m, $v_y = +5.2$ m/s (above the railing, still rising). At $t = 4.00$ s: $y = -18.4$ m, $v_y = -24.2$ m/s (below the railing, falling).\n3. (b) No time given, so use $v_y^2 = v_{0y}^2 - 2g\\,y = 225 - 2(9.80)(5.00) = 127$ m²/s², so $v_y = \\pm 11.3$ m/s: +11.3 on the way up, -11.3 on the way down.\n4. (c) At the top $v_y = 0$: $y_1 = v_{0y}^2/(2g) = 225/19.6 = 11.5$ m.\n5. (d) At the top $a_y = -g = -9.80$ m/s², still downward. Zero velocity does not mean zero acceleration.","answers":["(a) t = 1.00 s: y = +10.1 m, v = +5.2 m/s; t = 4.00 s: y = -18.4 m, v = -24.2 m/s","(b) ±11.3 m/s (up on the way up, down on the way down)","(c) 11.5 m above the railing","(d) -9.80 m/s² (9.80 m/s² downward)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 2.7"},{"id":"2.8","title":"Time for a thrown ball to fall below its launch point","q":"A ball is thrown vertically upward at 15.0 m/s from a point even with a roof railing, and is then in free fall (g = 9.80 m/s²). At what time after leaving the hand is the ball 5.00 m below the roof railing?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: origin at the railing, upward positive; $y_0 = 0$, $v_{0y} = 15.0$ m/s, $a_y = -9.80$ m/s². The target is $t$ when $y = -5.00$ m.\n2. Relation: $y = v_{0y}t - \\tfrac12 g t^2$, i.e. $-5.00 = 15.0\\,t - 4.90\\,t^2$.\n3. Rearrange to a quadratic: $4.90\\,t^2 - 15.0\\,t - 5.00 = 0$.\n4. Quadratic formula: $t = [15.0 \\pm \\sqrt{225 + 98.0}]/9.80 = [15.0 \\pm 17.97]/9.80$, so $t = 3.36$ s or $t = -0.303$ s.\n5. Interpretation: the negative root belongs to the trajectory extrapolated backward in time: a ball moving upward at 17.97 m/s from a point 5.00 m below the railing at $t = -0.303$ s would pass the railing at 15.0 m/s at $t=0$. It is not physical for this throw. Only $t = 3.36$ s applies.","answers":["t = 3.36 s (the root t = -0.30 s is rejected)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 2.8"},{"id":"2.9","title":"Car with time-varying acceleration","q":"Sally is driving along a straight highway in her 1965 Mustang. At t = 0, when she is moving at 10 m/s in the positive x-direction, she passes a signpost at x = 50 m. Her x-acceleration as a function of time is aₓ = 2.0 m/s² − (0.10 m/s³)t. (a) Find her x-velocity vₓ and position x as functions of time. (b) When is her x-velocity greatest? (c) What is that maximum x-velocity? (d) Where is the car when it reaches that maximum x-velocity?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: acceleration depends on $t$, so the constant-acceleration equations do not apply; integrate $a_x$ and then $v_x$.\n2. (a) $v_x = v_{0x} + \\int_0^t a_x\\,dt\u0027 = 10 + 2.0t - 0.050\\,t^2$ (m/s). Then $x = x_0 + \\int_0^t v_x\\,dt\u0027 = 50 + 10t + 1.0\\,t^2 - \\tfrac{0.050}{3}t^3$ (m), i.e. $x = 50 + 10t + t^2 - 0.0167\\,t^3$.\n3. (b) $v_x$ is greatest when $a_x = 0$ (it increases before and decreases after): $2.0 - 0.10\\,t = 0$, so $t = 20$ s.\n4. (c) $v_{max} = 10 + 2.0(20) - 0.050(20)^2 = 10 + 40 - 20 = 30$ m/s.\n5. (d) $x(20) = 50 + 200 + 400 - 0.0167(8000) = 50 + 200 + 400 - 133 \\approx 517$ m.\n6. Check: for $t \u003e 20$ s, $a_x \u003c 0$ and the car slows; $v_x = 0$ again at about 44.5 s.","answers":["(a) v_x = 10 m/s + (2.0 m/s²)t − (0.050 m/s³)t²; x = 50 m + (10 m/s)t + (1.0 m/s²)t² − (0.0167 m/s³)t³","(b) t = 20 s","(c) 30 m/s","(d) about 517 m"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 2.9"},{"id":"3.1","title":"Mars rover position, displacement and velocity vectors","q":"A robotic vehicle, or rover, is exploring the surface of Mars. The stationary Mars lander is the origin of coordinates, and the surrounding Martian surface lies in the xy-plane. The rover, which we represent as a point, has x- and y-coordinates that vary with time: x = 2.0 m − (0.25 m/s²)t² and y = (1.0 m/s)t + (0.025 m/s³)t³. (a) Find the rover\u0027s coordinates and distance from the lander at t = 2.0 s. (b) Find the rover\u0027s displacement and average velocity vectors for the interval t = 0.0 s to t = 2.0 s. (c) Find a general expression for the rover\u0027s instantaneous velocity vector v. Express v at t = 2.0 s in component form and in terms of magnitude and direction.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: position $\\vec r = x\\hat\\imath + y\\hat\\jmath$; displacement $\\Delta\\vec r = \\vec r_2 - \\vec r_1$; $\\vec v_{av} = \\Delta\\vec r/\\Delta t$; $\\vec v = d\\vec r/dt$ with components $v_x = dx/dt$, $v_y = dy/dt$.\n2. (a) At $t=2.0$ s: $x = 2.0 - 0.25(4.0) = 1.0$ m; $y = 1.0(2.0) + 0.025(8.0) = 2.2$ m; $r = \\sqrt{x^2+y^2} = \\sqrt{1.0^2 + 2.2^2} = 2.4$ m.\n3. (b) At $t=0$: $x = 2.0$ m, $y = 0$. So $\\Delta\\vec r = (1.0-2.0)\\hat\\imath + (2.2-0)\\hat\\jmath = (-1.0\\ \\text{m})\\hat\\imath + (2.2\\ \\text{m})\\hat\\jmath$. Dividing by $\\Delta t = 2.0$ s: $\\vec v_{av} = (-0.50\\ \\text{m/s})\\hat\\imath + (1.1\\ \\text{m/s})\\hat\\jmath$.\n4. (c) $v_x = dx/dt = -(0.50\\ \\text{m/s}^2)t$, $v_y = dy/dt = 1.0\\ \\text{m/s} + (0.075\\ \\text{m/s}^3)t^2$.\n5. At $t=2.0$ s: $v_x = -1.0$ m/s, $v_y = 1.0 + 0.075(4.0) = 1.3$ m/s, so $\\vec v = (-1.0\\ \\text{m/s})\\hat\\imath + (1.3\\ \\text{m/s})\\hat\\jmath$.\n6. Magnitude $v = \\sqrt{1.0^2+1.3^2} = 1.6$ m/s. Direction: $\\tan\\alpha = v_y/v_x = -1.3$; since $v_x\u003c0, v_y\u003e0$, $\\alpha = 180^\\circ - 52^\\circ = 128^\\circ$ from $+x$.\n7. Check: the instantaneous velocity points tangent to the path, differs from the average velocity over 0 to 2 s.","answers":["(a) x = 1.0 m, y = 2.2 m, r = 2.4 m","(b) Δr = (−1.0 m)i + (2.2 m)j; v_av = (−0.50 m/s)i + (1.1 m/s)j","(c) v_x = −(0.50 m/s²)t, v_y = 1.0 m/s + (0.075 m/s³)t²; at 2.0 s: v = (−1.0 m/s)i + (1.3 m/s)j, magnitude 1.6 m/s, direction 128° from +x"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 3.1"},{"id":"3.7","title":"Batted baseball: position, apex and range","q":"A batter hits a baseball so that it leaves the bat at speed v₀ = 37.0 m/s at an angle α₀ = 53.1° above the horizontal. Ignore air resistance and the height of the bat above the ground (assume the ball starts at ground level, y₀ = 0). (a) Find the position of the ball and its velocity (magnitude and direction) at t = 2.00 s. (b) Find the time when the ball reaches the highest point of its flight, and its height h at this time. (c) Find the horizontal range R, that is, the horizontal distance from the starting point to where the ball hits the ground. Use g = 9.80 m/s².","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: projectile motion, $a_x=0$, $a_y=-g$. Initial components: $v_{0x} = v_0\\cos\\alpha_0 = 22.2$ m/s, $v_{0y} = v_0\\sin\\alpha_0 = 29.6$ m/s.\n2. (a) At $t = 2.00$ s: $x = v_{0x}t = 44.4$ m; $y = v_{0y}t - \\tfrac12 g t^2 = 59.2 - 19.6 = 39.6$ m. Velocity: $v_x = 22.2$ m/s, $v_y = v_{0y} - gt = 29.6 - 19.6 = 10.0$ m/s (still rising).\n3. Speed $v = \\sqrt{22.2^2+10.0^2} = 24.4$ m/s; direction $\\alpha = \\arctan(10.0/22.2) = 24.2^\\circ$ above the horizontal.\n4. (b) Apex where $v_y = 0$: $t_1 = v_{0y}/g = 29.6/9.80 = 3.02$ s. Height $h = v_{0y}t_1 - \\tfrac12 g t_1^2 = 44.7$ m.\n5. (c) The ball returns to ground level when $y = 0$: $t_2 = 2v_{0y}/g = 2t_1 = 6.04$ s (the other root is $t=0$). Then $R = v_{0x}t_2 = (22.2)(6.04) = 134$ m.\n6. Check: symmetry of the parabola gives $t_2 = 2t_1$ and the apex at $x = R/2$.","answers":["(a) x = 44.4 m, y = 39.6 m; speed 24.4 m/s at 24.2° above the horizontal (v_x = 22.2 m/s, v_y = 10.0 m/s)","(b) t = 3.02 s, h = 44.7 m","(c) R = 134 m (flight time 6.04 s)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 3.7"},{"id":"3.8","title":"General height and range; best launch angles","q":"Find the maximum height h and the horizontal range R of a projectile launched with speed v₀ at an initial angle α₀ between 0° and 90°, launched from and landing at the same height, with no air resistance. For a given v₀, what value of α₀ gives the maximum height? What value gives the maximum horizontal range?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: use the components $v_{0x} = v_0\\cos\\alpha_0$ and $v_{0y} = v_0\\sin\\alpha_0$ with $a_y = -g$. Take the launch point as the origin.\n2. Time to the apex ($v_y=0$): $t_1 = v_{0y}/g = v_0\\sin\\alpha_0/g$.\n3. Height: $h = v_{0y}t_1 - \\tfrac12 g t_1^2 = \\dfrac{v_0^2\\sin^2\\alpha_0}{2g}$.\n4. Flight time back to the starting height: $t_2 = 2t_1 = 2v_0\\sin\\alpha_0/g$.\n5. Range: $R = v_0\\cos\\alpha_0\\,t_2 = \\dfrac{2v_0^2\\sin\\alpha_0\\cos\\alpha_0}{g} = \\dfrac{v_0^2\\sin 2\\alpha_0}{g}$.\n6. Maximum $h$ occurs when $\\sin\\alpha_0 = 1$, i.e. $\\alpha_0 = 90^\\circ$ (straight up). Maximum $R$ occurs when $\\sin2\\alpha_0 = 1$, i.e. $\\alpha_0 = 45^\\circ$, giving $R_{max} = v_0^2/g$.\n7. Check: complementary angles ($\\alpha_0$ and $90^\\circ - \\alpha_0$) give the same range since $\\sin 2\\alpha_0 = \\sin(180^\\circ - 2\\alpha_0)$. These formulas apply only when launch and landing heights are equal.","answers":["h = v₀² sin²α₀ / (2g)","R = v₀² sin(2α₀) / g","Maximum height at α₀ = 90°; maximum range at α₀ = 45° (R_max = v₀²/g)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 3.8"},{"id":"3.9","title":"Ball thrown downward from a window","q":"You throw a ball from your window 8.0 m above the ground. When the ball leaves your hand, it is moving at 10.0 m/s at an angle of 20° below the horizontal. How far horizontally from your window will the ball hit the ground? Ignore air resistance. Use g = 9.80 m/s².","figs":["figures/ex-phys/ex3-9.webp"],"figDesc":"Axes with origin at the window (the point where the ball leaves your hand), x horizontal and y upward. The initial velocity is 10.0 m/s directed 20° below the +x axis (α₀ = −20°); the ground is at y = −8.0 m; the horizontal distance x to the landing point is unknown.","sol":"1. Approach: projectile motion with origin at the release point; $v_0 = 10.0$ m/s, $\\alpha_0 = -20^\\circ$, landing at $y = -8.0$ m. Find the time first, then $x$.\n2. $y = (v_0\\sin\\alpha_0)t - \\tfrac12 g t^2$ gives the quadratic $\\tfrac12 g t^2 - (v_0\\sin\\alpha_0)t + y = 0$, i.e. $4.90\\,t^2 + 3.42\\,t - 8.0 = 0$ (since $v_0\\sin\\alpha_0 = -3.42$ m/s).\n3. Roots: $t = [-3.42 \\pm \\sqrt{3.42^2 + 4(4.90)(8.0)}]/9.80 = [-3.42 \\pm 12.98]/9.80$, i.e. $t = 0.98$ s or $t = -1.7$ s.\n4. Reject the negative root (before the throw). $t = 0.98$ s.\n5. Horizontal distance: $x = (v_0\\cos\\alpha_0)t = (10.0)(\\cos 20^\\circ)(0.98) = 9.2$ m.\n6. Check: the fall time without the initial downward speed would be $\\sqrt{2(8.0)/9.8} = 1.28$ s, so 0.98 s is reasonable.","answers":["x ≈ 9.2 m from the window (time of flight 0.98 s)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 3.9"},{"id":"3.10","title":"Dart fired at a falling monkey","q":"A monkey escapes from the zoo and climbs a tree. After failing to entice the monkey down, the zookeeper fires a tranquilizer dart directly at the monkey, aiming along the straight line from the gun muzzle to the monkey\u0027s initial position (horizontal distance d, so the line is at launch angle α₀ above the horizontal and the monkey is initially at height d tanα₀). The monkey lets go at the instant the dart leaves the gun. Show that the dart will always hit the monkey, provided that the dart reaches the monkey before he hits the ground and runs away. Ignore air resistance.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: both bodies undergo projectile motion starting at the same instant. Origin at the gun muzzle; the dart has $v_0$ at angle $\\alpha_0$; the monkey starts at $(d,\\ d\\tan\\alpha_0)$ at rest.\n2. Same $x$: the monkey drops straight down, so $x_{monkey} = d$ always. The dart has $x_{dart} = (v_0\\cos\\alpha_0)t$. They are equal at $t = d/(v_0\\cos\\alpha_0)$.\n3. Monkey\u0027s height: $y_{monkey} = d\\tan\\alpha_0 - \\tfrac12 g t^2$.\n4. Dart\u0027s height: $y_{dart} = (v_0\\sin\\alpha_0)t - \\tfrac12 g t^2$. At $t = d/(v_0\\cos\\alpha_0)$, $(v_0\\sin\\alpha_0)t = d\\tan\\alpha_0$, so $y_{dart} = d\\tan\\alpha_0 - \\tfrac12 g t^2 = y_{monkey}$.\n5. So at the time their $x$-coordinates coincide, their $y$-coordinates also coincide: the dart hits the monkey, for any $v_0$ and $\\alpha_0$ (as long as that happens before the ground).\n6. Insight: relative to free fall, both fall the same distance $\\tfrac12 g t^2$ below where they would be without gravity, so the aim along the line of sight works with or without gravity.","answers":["Hit at t = d/(v₀ cos α₀), when both have height d tan α₀ − ½gt²; the dart always hits the monkey if it reaches him before he reaches the ground"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 3.10"},{"id":"3.15","title":"Heading a plane to fly due north in a crosswind","q":"An airplane flies with an airspeed (speed relative to the air) of 240 km/h. There is a 100-km/h wind blowing from west to east (that is, the air moves due east relative to the earth). In what direction should the pilot head to travel due north relative to the earth? What will be her velocity (speed) relative to the earth?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: relative velocity: $\\vec v_{P/E} = \\vec v_{P/A} + \\vec v_{A/E}$ (plane relative to earth = plane relative to air + air relative to earth).\n2. Known: $\\vec v_{A/E}$ = 100 km/h due east; $|\\vec v_{P/A}|$ = 240 km/h, direction unknown (angle $\\beta$); $\\vec v_{P/E}$ must point due north, magnitude unknown.\n3. The three vectors form a right triangle with hypotenuse $v_{P/A}$ (240 km/h), because $\\vec v_{P/E}$ (north) is perpendicular to $\\vec v_{A/E}$ (east).\n4. The east components cancel: $v_{P/A}\\sin\\beta = v_{A/E}$, so $\\sin\\beta = 100/240$ and $\\beta = 24.6^\\circ$. The nose must point $24.6^\\circ$ west of north (into the wind).\n5. Ground speed: $v_{P/E} = \\sqrt{240^2 - 100^2} = 218$ km/h, due north.\n6. Check: the crosswind has to be cancelled by part of the airspeed, so the ground speed (218 km/h) is lower than the airspeed (240 km/h). Compare with Example 3.14 where heading north gives 260 km/h, 23° east of north.","answers":["Head 24.6° west of north (sin β = 100/240)","Ground speed 218 km/h, due north"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 3.15"},{"id":"4.1","title":"Three wrestlers pulling on a belt","q":"Three professional wrestlers are fighting over a champion\u0027s belt. Seen from above, the horizontal forces they apply to the belt are: F₁ = 250 N, directed up and to the left at an angle of 53° above the −x axis (that is, at 127° from the +x axis); F₂ = 50 N, directed along the +x axis; and F₃ = 120 N, directed along the −y axis (270° from +x). Find the x- and y-components of the net force on the belt, and find its magnitude and direction.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure; the directions of the three forces are given in the statement)","sol":"1. Approach: superposition of forces; use the component method $R_x = \\sum F_x$, $R_y = \\sum F_y$.\n2. Angles from +x: $\\theta_1 = 180^\\circ - 53^\\circ = 127^\\circ$, $\\theta_2 = 0^\\circ$, $\\theta_3 = 270^\\circ$.\n3. $F_{1x} = 250\\cos127^\\circ = -150$ N; $F_{1y} = 250\\sin127^\\circ = +200$ N.\n4. $F_{2x} = 50$ N; $F_{2y} = 0$. $F_{3x} = 0$; $F_{3y} = -120$ N.\n5. $R_x = -150 + 50 + 0 = -100$ N; $R_y = 200 + 0 - 120 = +80$ N.\n6. Magnitude $R = \\sqrt{(-100)^2 + 80^2} = 128$ N.\n7. Direction: $\\tan\\theta = R_y/R_x = -0.80$; since $R_x\u003c0$, $R_y\u003e0$ the vector lies in the second quadrant, $\\theta = 180^\\circ - 39^\\circ = 141^\\circ$ from the +x axis.\n8. Check: the net force points up and to the left, dominated by the 250 N wrestler, partly cancelled by the other two.","answers":["R_x = −100 N, R_y = +80 N","R = 128 N, at θ = 141° measured from the +x axis (second quadrant)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 4.1"},{"id":"4.3","title":"Net force on a fast car and a slow car","q":"You are driving a Maserati GranTurismo S on a straight testing track at a constant speed of 250 km/h. You pass a 1971 Volkswagen Beetle doing a constant 75 km/h. On which car is the net force greater? Explain, and say which forces act on each car.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Key word: net. Both cars move at constant velocity, so both are in equilibrium.\n2. Newton\u0027s first law: with constant velocity ($\\vec a = 0$), the net force is zero on each car.\n3. The common-sense idea that the faster car needs a bigger pushing force is about the forward force alone. The track pushes the Maserati forward harder (its engine is more powerful) than the Beetle, but a backward force (road friction plus air resistance, which grows with speed) also acts on each car.\n4. For each car the forward and backward forces sum to zero: more air resistance on the fast Maserati balances a larger forward force; less on the Beetle.\n5. Conclusion: the net force is zero on both cars; neither is greater.","answers":["The net force is zero on both cars (constant velocity); the Maserati has larger forward and backward forces, but they cancel"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Conceptual Example 4.3"},{"id":"4.4","title":"Box pushed on a frictionless floor","q":"A worker applies a constant horizontal force of magnitude 20 N to a box with mass 40 kg resting on a level floor with negligible friction. What is the acceleration of the box? Also find the magnitude of the normal force the floor exerts on the box (g = 9.80 m/s²).","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: Newton\u0027s second law in components. Take +x along the applied force and +y upward. Forces on the box: the 20 N push (horizontal), its weight $w = mg$ (down), and the normal force $n$ (up).\n2. Horizontal: only the push has an x-component, so $\\sum F_x = 20$ N $= m a_x$.\n3. $a_x = 20\\text{ N}/40\\text{ kg} = 0.50$ m/s², in the +x direction (same as the net force).\n4. Vertical: no vertical acceleration, $\\sum F_y = n - mg = 0$, so $n = mg = (40)(9.80) = 392$ N, equal to the weight.\n5. Since the net force is constant, the acceleration is constant and the Chapter 2 formulas apply.","answers":["a = 0.50 m/s² in the direction of the applied force","n = mg ≈ 392 N"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 4.4"},{"id":"4.5","title":"Friction force on a sliding ketchup bottle","q":"A waitress shoves a ketchup bottle with mass 0.45 kg to her right along a smooth, level lunch counter. The bottle leaves her hand moving at 2.8 m/s, then slows down as it slides because of a constant horizontal friction force exerted on it by the countertop. It slides for 1.0 m before coming to rest. What are the magnitude and direction of the friction force acting on the bottle?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: kinematics gives the acceleration, then Newton\u0027s second law gives the force. Take +x in the direction of sliding, origin at the release point.\n2. Known: $v_{0x} = 2.8$ m/s, $v_x = 0$ after $x = 1.0$ m. The friction force is constant, so the acceleration is constant.\n3. Use $v_x^2 = v_{0x}^2 + 2a_x(x - x_0)$: $a_x = -v_{0x}^2/(2x) = -(2.8)^2/(2 \\cdot 1.0) = -3.92$ m/s² (toward the left, opposite the motion).\n4. Newton\u0027s second law (the only horizontal force is friction): $-f = m a_x$, so $f_x = (0.45\\text{ kg})(-3.92\\text{ m/s}^2) = -1.8$ N.\n5. The friction force has magnitude 1.8 N and points opposite to the bottle\u0027s velocity (to the left).\n6. Check: $f/mg = 1.8/4.4 \\approx 0.4$, a reasonable friction coefficient for a counter.","answers":["Friction force magnitude 1.8 N, directed opposite to the motion (to the left)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 4.5"},{"id":"4.7","title":"Emergency stop of a heavy car","q":"A Rolls-Royce Phantom of weight 2.49 × 10⁴ N, traveling in the +x direction, makes an emergency stop; the x-component of the net force acting on it is −1.83 × 10⁴ N. What is its acceleration? Express it also as a multiple of g (g = 9.80 m/s²).","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: Newton\u0027s second law $\\sum F_x = m a_x$ needs the mass; the given 2.49 × 10⁴ N is a weight, not a mass.\n2. Mass from weight: $m = w/g = 2.49\\times10^4\\text{ N}/9.80\\text{ m/s}^2 = 2540$ kg.\n3. Acceleration: $a_x = \\sum F_x/m = (-1.83\\times10^4\\text{ N})/(2540\\text{ kg}) = -7.20$ m/s².\n4. In units of $g$: $a_x/g = -7.20/9.80 = -0.735$; equivalently $\\sum F_x/w = -1.83/2.49 = -0.735$, since the net force divided by the weight equals the acceleration expressed in $g$.\n5. The negative sign means the acceleration points in the −x direction, opposite the velocity, so the car slows down. The magnitude is high; passengers feel strong belt forces.","answers":["a = −7.20 m/s² (7.20 m/s² opposite to the velocity)","a = −0.735 g; the mass is 2540 kg"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 4.7"},{"id":"4.10","title":"Mason pulling a block by a rope: force pairs","q":"A stonemason drags a marble block across a floor by pulling on a rope attached to the block. The block is not necessarily in equilibrium. How are the various forces related, and what are the action-reaction pairs? Use B for the block, R for the rope and M for the mason, and say when the force of the mason on the rope and the force of the block on the rope are equal in magnitude.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Third-law pairs: the force of the mason on the rope and the force of the rope on the mason, $\\vec F_{M\\,on\\,R} = -\\vec F_{R\\,on\\,M}$; and the force of the block on the rope and of the rope on the block, $\\vec F_{B\\,on\\,R} = -\\vec F_{R\\,on\\,B}$.\n2. $\\vec F_{M\\,on\\,R}$ and $\\vec F_{B\\,on\\,R}$ are not an action-reaction pair: both act on the same body (the rope).\n3. Second law for the rope: $\\vec F_{M\\,on\\,R} + \\vec F_{B\\,on\\,R} = m_{rope}\\vec a_{rope}$. If the rope accelerates, the two forces differ in magnitude.\n4. Special case 1: rope in equilibrium ($\\vec a_{rope} = 0$): $\\vec F_{B\\,on\\,R} = -\\vec F_{M\\,on\\,R}$. This follows from the first or second law, not the third.\n5. Special case 2: massless rope ($m_{rope} \\approx 0$): same result even while accelerating. The rope then transmits the mason\u0027s force to the block: $F_{R\\,on\\,B} = F_{M\\,on\\,R}$.\n6. The tension is the magnitude of the force at any point; it is the same throughout an equilibrium rope or a massless rope.","answers":["Pairs: F(M on R) = −F(R on M) and F(B on R) = −F(R on B); F(M on R) and F(B on R) are not a pair","F(M on R) and F(B on R) are equal in magnitude only if the rope is in equilibrium or massless"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Conceptual Example 4.10"},{"id":"5.3","title":"Engine hanging from three chains","q":"A car engine with weight w hangs from a chain that is linked at a ring O to two other chains, one fastened to the ceiling and the other to the wall. The chain to the ceiling makes an angle of 60° with the ceiling (as shown); the other is horizontal. Find expressions for the tension in each of the three chains in terms of w. The weights of the ring and chains are negligible compared with the weight of the engine.","figs":["figures/ex-phys/ex5-3.webp"],"figDesc":"A car engine hangs from a vertical chain (tension T₁) attached to a ring O. A horizontal chain (T₂) runs from O to the wall on the left; a slanted chain (T₃) runs from O up to the ceiling, making a 60° angle with the ceiling (the horizontal) at the ceiling.","sol":"1. Approach: everything is in equilibrium, so apply Newton\u0027s first law to the engine and to the ring O (two bodies, because one body gives only two equations for three unknowns).\n2. Engine: $\\sum F_y = T_1 - w = 0$, so $T_1 = w$.\n3. Ring O: forces are $T_1$ (down, the chain pulling on the ring), $T_2$ (horizontal, toward the wall, to the left) and $T_3$ (up and to the right at 60° from the horizontal). The ring\u0027s weight is negligible.\n4. $\\sum F_x = T_3\\cos60^\\circ - T_2 = 0$ and $\\sum F_y = T_3\\sin60^\\circ - T_1 = 0$.\n5. From the y-equation: $T_3 = T_1/\\sin60^\\circ = w/\\sin60^\\circ = 1.2\\,w$.\n6. From the x-equation: $T_2 = T_3\\cos60^\\circ = w\\cos60^\\circ/\\sin60^\\circ = 0.58\\,w$.\n7. Check: $T_3$ is the largest tension (the chain most likely to break), and it must exceed $w$ because it supports the engine\u0027s weight with only its vertical component.","answers":["T₁ = w","T₂ = w cot 60° = 0.58 w","T₃ = w / sin 60° = 1.2 w"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 5.3"},{"id":"5.8","title":"Cable tension while an elevator comes to rest","q":"An elevator and its load have a combined mass of 800 kg. The elevator is initially moving downward at 10.0 m/s; it slows to a stop with constant acceleration in a distance of 25.0 m. What is the tension T in the supporting cable while the elevator is being brought to rest? Use g = 9.80 m/s².","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: use kinematics for the acceleration, then Newton\u0027s second law for the cable tension. Take +y upward.\n2. Initial y-velocity $v_{0y} = -10.0$ m/s, displacement $y - y_0 = -25.0$ m, final $v_y = 0$.\n3. $v_y^2 = v_{0y}^2 + 2a_y(y - y_0)$ gives $a_y = \\dfrac{0 - (-10.0)^2}{2(-25.0)} = +2.00$ m/s² (upward: the elevator is slowing while moving down).\n4. Free-body diagram: tension $T$ upward, weight $mg$ downward. $\\sum F_y = T - mg = ma_y$.\n5. $T = m(g + a_y) = (800)(9.80 + 2.00) = 9440$ N.\n6. Check: $T$ exceeds the weight (7840 N) because the net force must point upward. The same tension would be needed for an elevator moving up and speeding up at 2.00 m/s².","answers":["T = 9.44 × 10³ N (the acceleration is 2.00 m/s² upward; the weight is 7840 N)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 5.8"},{"id":"5.12","title":"Glider and hanging weight over a pulley","q":"An air-track glider with mass m₁ moves on a level, frictionless air track in the physics lab. The glider is connected to a lab weight with mass m₂ by a light, flexible, non-stretching string that passes over a stationary, frictionless pulley at the end of the track, so that the weight hangs vertically. Find the acceleration of each body and the tension in the string.","figs":["figures/ex-phys/ex5-12.webp"],"figDesc":"Glider of mass m₁ on a horizontal air track, connected by a string over a pulley at the end of the track to a weight of mass m₂ hanging vertically.","sol":"1. Approach: two bodies with unknowns $T$, $a_{1x}$, $a_{2y}$. Draw a free-body diagram for each. Take +x for the glider toward the pulley and +y downward for the weight, so both accelerations are positive.\n2. Constraint: the string does not stretch, so the two bodies have the same acceleration magnitude, $a_{1x} = a_{2y} = a$. The string is light and the pulley frictionless, so the tension is the same on both bodies.\n3. Glider: $\\sum F_x = T = m_1 a$ (the normal force cancels its weight vertically).\n4. Weight (+y down): $\\sum F_y = m_2 g - T = m_2 a$.\n5. Add the two equations: $m_2 g = (m_1 + m_2)a$, so $a = \\dfrac{m_2}{m_1 + m_2}\\,g$.\n6. Substitute: $T = \\dfrac{m_1m_2}{m_1+m_2}\\,g$.\n7. Checks: $T \u003c m_2 g$ because the weight accelerates. For $m_1\\gg m_2$, $a\\to 0$ and $T\\to m_2 g$; for $m_1\\to0$, $a\\to g$ and $T\\to0$ (free fall).","answers":["a₁ₓ = a₂ᵧ = m₂ g /(m₁ + m₂)","T = m₁ m₂ g /(m₁ + m₂), less than m₂ g"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 5.12"},{"id":"5.15","title":"Pulling a crate at an angle with friction","q":"A crate of weight 500 N rests on a level floor. You move the crate at constant velocity by pulling on a rope attached to it, with the rope directed upward at an angle of 30° above the horizontal. The coefficient of kinetic friction between the crate and floor is μk = 0.40. How hard must you pull on the rope? What is the magnitude of the normal force on the crate?","figs":["figures/ex-phys/ex5-15.webp"],"figDesc":"A person pulls a crate along a floor using a rope that makes a 30° angle above the horizontal.","sol":"1. Approach: constant velocity means equilibrium (Newton\u0027s first law). Since the crate moves, the floor exerts kinetic friction $f_k = \\mu_k n$, and the normal force is not equal to the weight because the rope has a vertical component.\n2. Axes: +x along the motion, +y upward. Forces: tension $T$ at 30°, friction $f_k$ (backward), normal $n$ (up), weight $w = 500$ N (down).\n3. $\\sum F_x = T\\cos30^\\circ - f_k = 0$, so $T\\cos30^\\circ = \\mu_k n$.\n4. $\\sum F_y = T\\sin30^\\circ + n - w = 0$, so $n = w - T\\sin30^\\circ$.\n5. Substitute: $T\\cos30^\\circ = \\mu_k(w - T\\sin30^\\circ)$, so $T = \\dfrac{\\mu_k w}{\\cos30^\\circ + \\mu_k\\sin30^\\circ} = \\dfrac{(0.40)(500)}{0.866 + 0.20} = 188$ N.\n6. $n = 500 - (188)(\\sin30^\\circ) = 406$ N.\n7. Check: the normal force is less than the weight, and the required pull (188 N) is less than the 200 N needed for a horizontal pull (since $\\mu_k w = 200$ N).","answers":["T ≈ 188 N","n ≈ 406 N (less than the 500 N weight)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 5.15"},{"id":"5.16","title":"Slope angle for constant-speed toboggan","q":"A toboggan with weight w slides down a hill that is inclined at angle α to the horizontal. The coefficient of kinetic friction between the toboggan and the snow is μk. The slope has just the right angle to make the toboggan slide with constant velocity. Find this angle in terms of w and μk.","figs":["figures/ex-phys/ex5-16.webp"],"figDesc":"A toboggan with riders slides down a straight slope inclined at angle α to the horizontal.","sol":"1. Approach: constant velocity means equilibrium. Choose x down the slope, y perpendicular to the slope (upward). Forces: weight $w$, normal force $n$ (perpendicular to the surface) and kinetic friction $f_k = \\mu_k n$ (up the slope, opposing the motion).\n2. Weight components: $w_x = w\\sin\\alpha$ (down the slope), $w_y = -w\\cos\\alpha$.\n3. $\\sum F_x = w\\sin\\alpha - \\mu_k n = 0$ and $\\sum F_y = n - w\\cos\\alpha = 0$.\n4. Therefore $n = w\\cos\\alpha$ and $\\mu_k w\\cos\\alpha = w\\sin\\alpha$.\n5. Divide: $\\mu_k = \\tan\\alpha$, so $\\alpha = \\arctan\\mu_k$.\n6. Weight cancels: any toboggan, whatever its weight, slides at constant speed on a slope with $\\tan\\alpha = \\mu_k$; a larger $\\mu_k$ needs a steeper slope.\n7. Check: for $\\alpha$ larger than this angle the toboggan accelerates with $a = g(\\sin\\alpha - \\mu_k\\cos\\alpha)$; for smaller angles it slows and stops.","answers":["α = arctan μk (independent of the weight w)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 5.16"},{"id":"5.20","title":"Tension and period of a conical pendulum","q":"An inventor designs a pendulum clock using a bob with mass m at the end of a thin wire of length L. Instead of swinging back and forth, the bob is to move in a horizontal circle with constant speed v, with the wire making a fixed angle β with the vertical direction. This is called a conical pendulum because the suspending wire traces out a cone. Find the tension F in the wire and the period T (the time for one revolution of the bob).","figs":["figures/ex-phys/ex5-20.webp"],"figDesc":"A bob of mass m hangs from a wire of length L fixed to the ceiling; the wire makes a constant angle β with the vertical, and the bob moves in a horizontal circle of radius R with speed v.","sol":"1. Approach: uniform circular motion in a horizontal plane. Forces on the bob: weight $mg$ (down) and tension $F$ along the wire. Take +x horizontally toward the center of the circle, +y up. The acceleration is $a_{rad}$ toward the center; no vertical acceleration.\n2. $\\sum F_x = F\\sin\\beta = m a_{rad}$ and $\\sum F_y = F\\cos\\beta - mg = 0$.\n3. From the y-equation: $F = mg/\\cos\\beta$.\n4. Divide the equations: $a_{rad} = g\\tan\\beta$.\n5. Relate to the period: $a_{rad} = 4\\pi^2R/T^2$ with $R = L\\sin\\beta$, so $T^2 = 4\\pi^2R/a_{rad}$ and $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\dfrac{R}{g\\tan\\beta}} = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\dfrac{L\\cos\\beta}{g}}$.\n6. Checks: as $\\beta$ increases, $\\cos\\beta$ decreases so the period gets shorter and the tension larger; $\\beta$ cannot reach $90^\\circ$ (it would need infinite tension and speed). For small $\\beta$, $T\\to2\\pi\\sqrt{L/g}$, the simple pendulum period.","answers":["F = mg / cos β","T = 2π √(L cos β / g)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 5.20"},{"id":"5.22","title":"Banking angle for a curve with no friction","q":"For a car traveling at a certain speed, it is possible to bank a curve at just the right angle so that no friction at all is needed to maintain the car\u0027s turning radius. Then a car can safely round the curve even on wet ice. An engineer plans to rebuild a curve of radius R so that a car moving at a chosen speed v can safely make the turn even with no friction. At what angle β should the curve be banked? Evaluate it for R = 230 m and v = 25 m/s (88 km/h). Use g = 9.8 m/s².","figs":["figures/ex-phys/ex5-22.webp"],"figDesc":"A car rounding a curve of radius R on a road banked at angle β to the horizontal; R is measured horizontally to the center of the circle.","sol":"1. Approach: with no friction, the only forces are weight $mg$ and the normal force $n$, perpendicular to the road surface, which is tilted by $\\beta$ from the horizontal; so $n$ has a horizontal component $n\\sin\\beta$ toward the center of the curve.\n2. Take +x horizontally toward the center, +y up. The acceleration is $a_{rad} = v^2/R$ in +x, with none vertical.\n3. $\\sum F_x = n\\sin\\beta = m a_{rad} = mv^2/R$ and $\\sum F_y = n\\cos\\beta - mg = 0$.\n4. From the second: $n = mg/\\cos\\beta$. Substituting: $\\tan\\beta = a_{rad}/g = v^2/(gR)$.\n5. $\\beta = \\arctan\\dfrac{v^2}{gR}$. Numerically: $\\beta = \\arctan\\dfrac{(25)^2}{(9.8)(230)} = \\arctan 0.277 = 15^\\circ$.\n6. Checks: the angle depends on speed and radius but not on the car\u0027s mass; at other speeds friction is needed. A tight turn at high speed (small $R$, large $v$) requires $\\beta\\to90^\\circ$ (airplanes bank the same way: $\\tan\\beta = v^2/gR$).","answers":["β = arctan(v²/(gR))","β ≈ 15° for R = 230 m and v = 25 m/s"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 5.22"},{"id":"5.23","title":"Passenger on a vertical Ferris wheel","q":"A passenger on a carnival Ferris wheel moves in a vertical circle of radius R with constant speed v. The seat remains upright during the motion. Find expressions for the force the seat exerts on the passenger (of mass m) at the top of the circle and at the bottom.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: uniform circular motion in a vertical circle. At the top the acceleration $v^2/R$ points downward (toward the center); at the bottom it points upward. The only forces: weight $mg$ (down) and the normal force from the seat $n$ (up).\n2. Take +y upward at both positions. Top: $a_y = -v^2/R$. $\\sum F_y = n_T - mg = -mv^2/R$, so $n_T = mg\\left(1 - \\dfrac{v^2}{gR}\\right)$.\n3. Bottom: $a_y = +v^2/R$. $\\sum F_y = n_B - mg = +mv^2/R$, so $n_B = mg\\left(1 + \\dfrac{v^2}{gR}\\right)$.\n4. Interpretation: the normal force is the apparent weight. At the top it is less than $mg$; at the bottom it is greater than $mg$.\n5. Special case: at the top, when $v = \\sqrt{gR}$ the seat force is zero and the passenger feels weightless (and $a = g$); for $v\u003e\\sqrt{gR}$ the seat could not push on the passenger, who would need a restraint.\n6. The speed is constant, so the net force always points toward the center and has constant magnitude $mv^2/R$.","answers":["Top: n_T = mg(1 − v²/(gR))","Bottom: n_B = mg(1 + v²/(gR))","Weightless at the top when v = √(gR)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 5.23"},{"id":"6.2","title":"Work done by several forces on a sled","q":"A farmer hitches her tractor to a sled loaded with firewood and pulls it a distance of 20 m along level ground. The total weight of sled and load is 14,700 N. The tractor exerts a constant 5000-N force at an angle of 36.9° above the horizontal. A 3500-N friction force opposes the sled\u0027s motion. Find the work done by each force acting on the sled and the total work done by all the forces.","figs":["figures/ex-phys/ex6-2.webp"],"figDesc":"A tractor pulls a sled of firewood by a chain; the chain makes angle φ above the horizontal (36.9°) with the direction of motion.","sol":"1. Each force is constant and the displacement is straight, so $W=Fs\\cos\\phi$ for each force; take +x along the motion.\n2. Weight and normal force are perpendicular to the displacement: $W_w=W_n=0$.\n3. Tractor: $W_T=(5000\\text{ N})(20\\text{ m})\\cos36.9^\\circ=(5000)(20)(0.800)=80\\text{ kJ}$.\n4. Friction is opposite to the motion ($\\phi=180^\\circ$): $W_f=-(3500)(20)=-70\\text{ kJ}$.\n5. Total work as the algebraic sum: $0+0+80-70=10$ kJ.\n6. Check with the net force: $\\sum F_x=5000\\cos36.9^\\circ-3500=500$ N, so $W_{tot}=(500\\text{ N})(20\\text{ m})=10$ kJ. The y-forces cancel and do no work.","answers":["W_weight = 0","W_normal = 0","W_tractor = +80 kJ","W_friction = −70 kJ","W_total = +10 kJ"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 6.2"},{"id":"6.4","title":"Forces on a pile-driver hammerhead","q":"The 200-kg steel hammerhead of a pile driver is lifted 3.00 m above the top of a vertical I-beam being driven into the ground. The hammerhead is then dropped, driving the I-beam 7.4 cm deeper into the ground. The vertical guide rails exert a constant 60-N friction force on the hammerhead. Use the work–energy theorem to find (a) the speed of the hammerhead just as it hits the I-beam and (b) the average force the hammerhead exerts on the I-beam. Ignore the effects of the air.","figs":["figures/ex-phys/ex6-4.webp"],"figDesc":"A pile-driver hammerhead hangs 3.00 m above the top of a vertical I-beam (point 1 at release, point 2 at first contact with the beam, point 3 where it stops 7.4 cm lower).","sol":"1. Apply the work–energy theorem twice: from point 1 (rest) to 2 (contact), then from 2 to 3 (rest).\n2. Weight $w=mg=(200)(9.8)=1960$ N, friction $f=60$ N upward, so the net downward force on the 1→2 leg is $w-f=1900$ N.\n3. $W_{tot}=(w-f)s_{12}=(1900)(3.00)=5700$ J $=\\tfrac12mv_2^2-0$, so $v_2=\\sqrt{2(5700)/200}=7.55$ m/s.\n4. On the 2→3 leg the beam pushes up with an average force $n$: $W_{tot}=(w-f-n)s_{23}=K_3-K_2=0-5700$ J, with $s_{23}=0.074$ m.\n5. $n=w-f-\\dfrac{K_3-K_2}{s_{23}}=1960-60+\\dfrac{5700}{0.074}=79{,}000$ N.\n6. By Newton\u0027s third law the hammerhead pushes down on the beam with the same 79 kN (over 40 times its weight).","answers":["(a) v = 7.55 m/s","(b) average force on the I-beam ≈ 79,000 N (about 79 kN)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 6.4"},{"id":"6.6","title":"Spring constant and work in a bathroom scale","q":"A woman weighing 600 N steps on a bathroom scale that contains a stiff spring. In equilibrium, the spring is compressed 1.0 cm under her weight. Find the force constant of the spring and the total work done on it during the compression.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. In equilibrium the spring force balances her weight. Take x positive for elongation (upward); the top of the spring moves $x=-1.0$ cm $=-0.010$ m and she exerts $F_x=-600$ N.\n2. Hooke\u0027s law $F_x=kx$ gives $k=\\dfrac{-600\\text{ N}}{-0.010\\text{ m}}=6.0\\times10^4$ N/m.\n3. Work done on the spring: $W=\\tfrac12kx_2^2-\\tfrac12kx_1^2$ with $x_1=0$, $x_2=-0.010$ m.\n4. $W=\\tfrac12(6.0\\times10^4)(0.010)^2-0=3.0$ J.\n5. The work is positive since force and displacement are in the same direction; the sign convention for x does not change k or W.","answers":["k = 6.0×10⁴ N/m","W = 3.0 J (positive work done on the spring)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 6.6"},{"id":"6.7","title":"Glider on a spring with and without friction","q":"An air-track glider of mass 0.100 kg is attached to the end of a horizontal air track by a spring with force constant 20.0 N/m. Initially the spring is unstretched and the glider is moving at 1.50 m/s to the right. Find the maximum distance d that the glider moves to the right (a) if the air track is turned on, so that there is no friction, and (b) if the air is turned off, so that there is kinetic friction with coefficient μk = 0.47.","figs":["figures/ex-phys/ex6-7.webp"],"figDesc":"A glider of mass m on a horizontal air track is attached to the end of the track by a spring of constant k; it moves to the right with speed v₁ and the spring is initially unstretched.","sol":"1. The spring force varies, so use the work–energy theorem. Take x = 0 at the unstretched position and x = d at the stopping point; the spring does work $-\\tfrac12kd^2$ on the glider, and the final K is zero.\n2. (a) No friction: $-\\tfrac12kd^2=0-\\tfrac12mv_1^2$, so $d=v_1\\sqrt{m/k}=(1.50)\\sqrt{0.100/20.0}=0.106$ m.\n3. (b) With friction $f_k=\\mu_kmg$ acts opposite to the motion, so $W_{fric}=-\\mu_kmgd$.\n4. $-\\mu_kmgd-\\tfrac12kd^2=-\\tfrac12mv_1^2$, i.e. $\\tfrac12kd^2+\\mu_kmgd-\\tfrac12mv_1^2=0$.\n5. Solve: $d=-\\dfrac{\\mu_kmg}{k}\\pm\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\mu_kmg}{k}\\right)^2+\\dfrac{mv_1^2}{k}}$ with $\\mu_kmg/k=0.02303$ m and $mv_1^2/k=0.01125$ m².\n6. The positive root is $d=0.086$ m. Friction shortens the travel, and $\\mu_k\\to0$ recovers part (a).","answers":["(a) d = 0.106 m = 10.6 cm","(b) d = 0.086 m = 8.6 cm"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 6.7"},{"id":"6.8","title":"Pushing a child in a swing quasi-statically","q":"At a family picnic you are appointed to push your cousin Throckmorton in a swing. His weight is w, the length of the chains is R, and you push Throcky until the chains make an angle θ₀ with the vertical. To do this, you exert a varying horizontal force F that starts at zero and gradually increases just enough that Throcky and the swing move very slowly and remain very nearly in equilibrium throughout the process. What is the total work done on Throcky by all forces? What is the work done by the tension T in the chains? What is the work you do by exerting the force F? (Neglect the weight of the chains and seat.)","figs":["figures/ex-phys/ex6-8.webp"],"figDesc":"A child sits in a swing with chains of length R, pushed by a horizontal force F; the chains make angle θ with the vertical and dl is an element of arc along the path (arc length s).","sol":"1. The motion is along a circular arc and the force varies, so use $W=\\int\\vec F\\cdot d\\vec l$.\n2. Equilibrium at every point means the net force is zero, so the total work is zero.\n3. The tension is perpendicular to $d\\vec l$ everywhere, so it does zero work.\n4. For your force: equilibrium gives $\\sum F_x=F-T\\sin\\theta=0$ and $\\sum F_y=T\\cos\\theta-w=0$, so $F=w\\tan\\theta$.\n5. With $ds=R\\,d\\theta$ and angle $\\theta$ between $\\vec F$ and $d\\vec l$: $W_F=\\int F\\cos\\theta\\,ds=\\int_0^{\\theta_0}(w\\tan\\theta)\\cos\\theta\\,R\\,d\\theta=wR\\int_0^{\\theta_0}\\sin\\theta\\,d\\theta$.\n6. $W_F=wR(1-\\cos\\theta_0)$.\n7. Check: gravity does $-wR(1-\\cos\\theta_0)$, so the sum is zero. $R(1-\\cos\\theta_0)$ is the height gained, so your work is weight times height gained.","answers":["Total work = 0","Work by tension = 0","Work by your force F = wR(1 − cos θ₀)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 6.8"},{"id":"6.9","title":"Power developed by jet engines in level flight","q":"Each of the four jet engines on an Airbus A380 airliner develops a thrust (a forward force on the airliner) of 322,000 N (72,000 lb). When the airplane is flying at 250 m/s (900 km/h, or roughly 560 mi/h), what horsepower does each engine develop?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Power is the rate at which the thrust does work, $P=\\vec F\\cdot\\vec v$.\n2. The thrust is along the velocity, so $F_\\parallel$ equals the thrust.\n3. $P=(3.22\\times10^5\\text{ N})(250\\text{ m/s})=8.05\\times10^7$ W.\n4. Convert with 1 hp = 746 W: $P=8.05\\times10^7/746\\approx108{,}000$ hp.\n5. Interpretation: at rest on the ground at full thrust the power would be zero, since $v=0$.","answers":["P = 8.05×10⁷ W ≈ 108,000 hp per engine"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 6.9"},{"id":"6.10","title":"Average power of a stair-climbing runner","q":"A 50.0-kg marathon runner runs up the stairs to the top of Chicago\u0027s 443-m-tall Willis Tower, the tallest building in the United States. To lift herself to the top in 15.0 minutes, what must be her average power output? Express your answer in watts, in kilowatts, and in horsepower.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Treat the runner as a particle lifted at constant speed against gravity.\n2. Method 1: work $W=mgh=(50.0)(9.80)(443)=2.17\\times10^5$ J done in $t=15.0\\text{ min}=900$ s, so $P_{av}=W/t=241$ W.\n3. Method 2: vertical speed $v=443/900=0.492$ m/s; force $mg$ along the motion, so $P_{av}=mg\\,v=(50.0)(9.80)(0.492)=241$ W.\n4. Convert: 241 W = 0.241 kW = 0.323 hp.\n5. This is only the useful power lifting her; her total physiological output (breathing, limb motion) is several times larger.","answers":["P_av ≈ 241 W","= 0.241 kW","= 0.323 hp"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 6.10"},{"id":"7.2","title":"Throwing a baseball: hand force and speed at a height","q":"You throw a 0.145-kg baseball straight up. Your hand moves upward by 0.50 m while you are throwing the ball, and the ball leaves your hand with an upward velocity of 20.0 m/s. (a) Find the magnitude of the force (assumed constant) that your hand exerts on the ball. (b) Find the speed of the ball at a point 15.0 m above the point where it leaves your hand. Ignore air resistance.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Take point 1 where the hand starts moving ($y_1=-0.50$ m, $v_1=0$), point 2 where the ball leaves the hand ($y_2=0$, $v_2=20.0$ m/s), point 3 at $y_3=15.0$ m. Only the hand does non-gravitational work, between 1 and 2.\n2. $K_1=0$, $U_1=mgy_1=(0.145)(9.80)(-0.50)=-0.71$ J; $K_2=\\tfrac12(0.145)(20.0)^2=29.0$ J, $U_2=0$.\n3. (a) $K_1+U_1+W_{other}=K_2+U_2$ gives $W_{other}=29.0-(-0.71)=29.7$ J. With constant force, $W_{other}=F(y_2-y_1)$, so $F=29.7/0.50=59$ N (over 40 times the ball\u0027s weight of 1.42 N).\n4. (b) From 2 to 3 only gravity acts, so $K_2+U_2=K_3+U_3$: $U_3=(0.145)(9.80)(15.0)=21.3$ J, so $K_3=29.0-21.3=7.7$ J.\n5. $v_3=\\sqrt{2K_3/m}=\\sqrt{2(7.7)/0.145}\\approx10$ m/s, whether rising or falling (speed only).","answers":["(a) F ≈ 59 N","(b) speed ≈ 10 m/s at 15.0 m above the release point (v = +10 m/s going up, −10 m/s coming down)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 7.2"},{"id":"7.4","title":"Skateboard on a frictionless quarter-circle ramp","q":"Throckmorton skateboards from rest down a curved, frictionless ramp. Treating him and his skateboard as a particle, he moves through a quarter-circle of radius R = 3.00 m. He and his skateboard have total mass 25.0 kg. (a) Find his speed at the bottom of the ramp. (b) Find the normal force that acts on him at the bottom of the curve.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Take point 1 at the top (at rest, $y_1=R$) and point 2 at the bottom ($y=0$). The normal force is perpendicular to his displacement everywhere, so only gravity does work and mechanical energy is conserved.\n2. (a) $K_1+U_1=K_2+U_2$: $0+mgR=\\tfrac12mv_2^2+0$, so $v_2=\\sqrt{2gR}=\\sqrt{2(9.80)(3.00)}=7.67$ m/s. This is independent of the ramp\u0027s shape; only the height R matters.\n3. (b) At the bottom he moves on a circle of radius R, so the acceleration is toward the center, upward, with $a_{rad}=v_2^2/R=2g$.\n4. $\\sum F_y=n-w=ma_{rad}=2mg$, so $n=w+2mg=3mg$.\n5. $n=3(25.0)(9.80)=735$ N, three times his weight, independent of R.","answers":["(a) v = 7.67 m/s","(b) n = 3mg = 735 N"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 7.4"},{"id":"7.6","title":"Crate on a rough incline: friction and return speed","q":"We want to slide a 12-kg crate up a 2.5-m-long ramp inclined at 30°. A worker, ignoring friction, calculates that he can do this by giving it an initial speed of 5.0 m/s at the bottom and letting it go. But friction is not negligible; the crate slides only 1.6 m up the ramp, stops, and slides back down. (a) Find the magnitude of the friction force acting on the crate, assuming that it is constant. (b) How fast is the crate moving when it reaches the bottom of the ramp?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Point 1 = bottom (take $y=0$, $v_1=5.0$ m/s); point 2 = highest point, $s=1.6$ m up the slope, $y_2=1.6\\sin30^\\circ=0.80$ m, $v_2=0$; point 3 = bottom again.\n2. (a) $K_1=\\tfrac12(12)(5.0)^2=150$ J, $U_1=0$, $K_2=0$, $U_2=(12)(9.8)(0.80)=94$ J.\n3. $K_1+U_1+W_{other}=K_2+U_2$ gives $W_{other}=-fs=94-150=-56$ J, so $f=56/1.6=35$ N.\n4. (b) The friction work on the way down is again $-56$ J, so over the round trip $W_{other}=-2fs=-112$ J.\n5. $K_3=K_1+U_1-U_3+W_{other}=150+0-0-112=38$ J.\n6. $v_3=\\sqrt{2K_3/m}=\\sqrt{2(38)/12}=2.5$ m/s, half the launch speed because only 38 J of the original 150 J remains.","answers":["(a) f = 35 N","(b) v₃ = 2.5 m/s"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 7.6"},{"id":"7.8","title":"Pushing a spring-loaded glider with a constant force","q":"A glider with mass 0.200 kg sits on a frictionless horizontal air track, connected to a spring with force constant 5.00 N/m. Initially the glider is at rest at x = 0 with the spring unstretched. You then push on the glider with a constant force of magnitude 0.610 N in the +x-direction. What is the glider\u0027s velocity when it has moved to x = 0.100 m?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. The spring force varies, and your force does non-spring work, so use $K_1+U_1+W_{other}=K_2+U_2$ with $U=\\tfrac12kx^2$ (no gravity change, horizontal track).\n2. Point 1: $x_1=0$, $K_1=0$, $U_1=0$. Point 2: $x_2=0.100$ m.\n3. Your force is constant and along the motion: $W_{other}=F\\Delta x=(0.610)(0.100)=0.0610$ J.\n4. $U_2=\\tfrac12(5.00)(0.100)^2=0.0250$ J.\n5. $K_2=K_1+U_1+W_{other}-U_2=0.0610-0.0250=0.0360$ J.\n6. $v_{2x}=\\sqrt{2K_2/m}=\\sqrt{2(0.0360)/0.200}=0.60$ m/s in the +x direction.\n7. Check: without the spring the glider would reach 0.78 m/s; the spring removes some energy.","answers":["v = +0.60 m/s (in the +x direction)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 7.8"},{"id":"7.9","title":"Cushioning spring for a falling test elevator","q":"A 2000-kg (19,600-N) elevator with broken cables in a test rig is falling at 4.00 m/s when it contacts a cushioning spring at the bottom of the shaft. The spring is intended to stop the elevator, compressing 2.00 m as it does so. During the motion a safety clamp applies a constant 17,000-N frictional force to the elevator. What is the necessary force constant k for the spring?","figs":["figures/ex-phys/ex7-9.webp"],"figDesc":"A 2000-kg elevator moves downward at v₁ = 4.00 m/s as it contacts a spring at the bottom of the shaft; it stops (v₂ = 0) after compressing the spring by 2.00 m (point 1 to point 2); a 17,000-N friction force acts on it upward.","sol":"1. Point 1 = elevator touches the spring (origin, $y_1=0$, $v_1=4.00$ m/s); point 2 = elevator stops at $y_2=-2.00$ m. Gravity, spring and friction act, so use $K_1+U_1+W_{other}=K_2+U_2$ with $U=mgy+\\tfrac12ky^2$.\n2. $K_1=\\tfrac12(2000)(4.00)^2=16{,}000$ J; $U_1=0$; $K_2=0$.\n3. $W_{other}=-f\\,s=-(17{,}000)(2.00)=-34{,}000$ J.\n4. $U_2=mgy_2+\\tfrac12ky_2^2=(19{,}600)(-2.00)+\\tfrac12k(2.00)^2=-39{,}200+2k$ (in joules, k in N/m).\n5. $16{,}000+0-34{,}000=0+(-39{,}200+2k)$, so $2k=21{,}200$.\n6. $k=1.06\\times10^4$ N/m. At full compression the spring force is $k(2.00)=21{,}200$ N, which exceeds the 19,600-N weight, so the net force on the stopped elevator is upward.","answers":["k = 1.06×10⁴ N/m"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 7.9"},{"id":"7.10","title":"Moving a futon: work depends on the path","q":"You are rearranging your furniture and wish to move a 40.0-kg futon 2.50 m across the room. A heavy coffee table, which you don\u0027t want to move, blocks this straight-line path. Instead, you slide the futon along a dogleg path; the doglegs are 2.00 m and 1.50 m long. How much more work must you do to push the futon along the dogleg path than along the straight-line path? The coefficient of kinetic friction is μk = 0.200. The futon starts and ends at rest.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. The futon is at rest at both ends ($K_1=K_2=0$), and it only moves horizontally ($U_1=U_2$), so the other work is zero: $W_{you}+W_{fric}=0$, i.e. $W_{you}=-W_{fric}$.\n2. The normal force equals the weight, so $f_k=\\mu_kmg$ and $W_{fric}=-f_ks$ for a path of length s; $W_{you}=\\mu_kmg\\,s$.\n3. Straight path: $W_{you}=(0.200)(40.0)(9.80)(2.50)=196$ J.\n4. Dogleg: $s=2.00+1.50=3.50$ m, $W_{you}=(0.200)(40.0)(9.80)(3.50)=274$ J.\n5. Extra work: $274-196=78$ J.\n6. Friction does different work between the same end points on different paths, so it is a nonconservative force.","answers":["Extra work = 78 J (274 J along the dogleg vs 196 J straight)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 7.10"},{"id":"7.11","title":"Is this position-dependent force conservative?","q":"In a region of space the force on an electron is F = Cx ĵ, where C is a positive constant. The electron moves around a square loop in the xy-plane with corners (0, 0), (L, 0), (L, L) and (0, L), traversed counterclockwise from the origin. Calculate the work done on the electron by the force F during a counterclockwise trip around the square. Is this force conservative or nonconservative?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Use $W=\\int\\vec F\\cdot d\\vec l$ on each leg, $\\vec F=Cx\\,\\hat\\jmath$.\n2. Leg 1, (0,0)→(L,0): $d\\vec l=dx\\,\\hat\\imath$ is perpendicular to $\\vec F$, so $\\vec F\\cdot d\\vec l=0$, $W_1=0$.\n3. Leg 2, (L,0)→(L,L): $x=L$ so $\\vec F=CL\\,\\hat\\jmath$ is constant and parallel to $d\\vec l=dy\\,\\hat\\jmath$: $W_2=CL\\int_0^Ldy=CL^2$.\n4. Leg 3, (L,L)→(0,L): $d\\vec l=dx\\,\\hat\\imath$, again perpendicular to $\\vec F$: $W_3=0$.\n5. Leg 4, (0,L)→(0,0): $x=0$, so $\\vec F=0$ and $W_4=0$.\n6. Round trip: $W=CL^2\\neq0$. A conservative force does zero work around a closed path, so this force is nonconservative and has no potential-energy function.\n7. Clockwise gives $-CL^2$ (unlike friction, which always removes energy).","answers":["W = CL² for the counterclockwise loop (−CL² clockwise)","The force is nonconservative (nonzero work around a closed path)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 7.11"},{"id":"8.3","title":"Impulse and average force on a kicked soccer ball","q":"A soccer ball has a mass of 0.40 kg. Initially it is moving to the left at 20 m/s but then it is kicked. After the kick it is moving at 45° upward and to the right with speed 30 m/s. Find the impulse of the net force and the average net force, assuming a collision time Δt = 0.010 s.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Treat momentum and impulse as vectors; take +x to the right and +y upward.\n2. Velocity components: before, $v_{1x}=-20$ m/s, $v_{1y}=0$. After, $v_{2x}=v_{2y}=(30)(0.707)=21.2$ m/s.\n3. Impulse components: $J_x=m(v_{2x}-v_{1x})=(0.40)(21.2+20)=16.5$ kg·m/s; $J_y=m(v_{2y}-v_{1y})=(0.40)(21.2)=8.5$ kg·m/s.\n4. Average force components: $(F_{av})_x=J_x/\\Delta t=1650$ N, $(F_{av})_y=J_y/\\Delta t=850$ N.\n5. Magnitude $F_{av}=\\sqrt{1650^2+850^2}=1.9\\times10^3$ N at $\\theta=\\arctan(850/1650)=27^\\circ$ above the +x axis.\n6. The final velocity (45°) is not along the average force because the ball was already moving. The ball\u0027s weight (3.9 N) is negligible during the kick.","answers":["Impulse J = (16.5 î + 8.5 ĵ) kg·m/s","Average force components: 1650 N (x) and 850 N (y)","F_av ≈ 1.9×10³ N at 27° above the +x axis"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 8.3"},{"id":"8.6","title":"Two robots collide on a frictionless surface","q":"Two battling robots move on a frictionless horizontal surface. Robot A, with mass 20 kg, initially moves at 2.0 m/s parallel to the x-axis. It collides with robot B, which has mass 12 kg and is initially at rest. After the collision, robot A moves at 1.0 m/s in a direction that makes an angle α = 30° with its initial direction (above the x-axis). What is the final velocity of robot B?","figs":["figures/ex-phys/ex8-6.webp"],"figDesc":"View from above: robot A at the origin moves along +x with velocity v_A1 toward robot B, which is at rest farther along the x-axis.","sol":"1. No horizontal external forces, so both $P_x$ and $P_y$ of the two-robot system are conserved.\n2. x: $m_Av_{A1x}+m_Bv_{B1x}=m_Av_{A2x}+m_Bv_{B2x}$, so $v_{B2x}=\\dfrac{(20)(2.0)+0-(20)(1.0)\\cos30^\\circ}{12}=1.89$ m/s.\n3. y: $0=m_Av_{A2y}+m_Bv_{B2y}$, so $v_{B2y}=\\dfrac{0-(20)(1.0)\\sin30^\\circ}{12}=-0.83$ m/s.\n4. Magnitude $v_{B2}=\\sqrt{1.89^2+0.83^2}=2.1$ m/s; direction $\\beta=\\arctan(-0.83/1.89)=-24^\\circ$ (below the x-axis).\n5. Check: final $P_x=17+23=40$ kg·m/s (initial 40) and $P_y=10-10=0$.","answers":["v_B2 = 2.1 m/s at 24° below the +x axis (components +1.89 m/s, −0.83 m/s)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 8.6"},{"id":"8.8","title":"Bullet speed from a ballistic pendulum","q":"A ballistic pendulum measures the speed of a bullet. A bullet of mass 5.00 g makes a completely inelastic collision with a block of wood of mass 2.00 kg, which is suspended like a pendulum by strings. After the impact, the block (with the bullet embedded) swings up to a maximum height y = 3.00 cm. Find the initial speed of the bullet, first in terms of the bullet mass, block mass and y, then numerically. Also find the speed of the block just after impact and compare the kinetic energies before and after the impact.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Two stages. Stage 1 (embedding): negligible external horizontal force, so horizontal momentum is conserved but mechanical energy is not. Stage 2 (swing): only gravity does work (tension does none), so mechanical energy is conserved but momentum is not.\n2. Stage 1: $m_Bv_1=(m_B+m_W)v_2$, so $v_1=\\dfrac{m_B+m_W}{m_B}v_2$.\n3. Stage 2: $\\tfrac12(m_B+m_W)v_2^2=(m_B+m_W)gy$, so $v_2=\\sqrt{2gy}$.\n4. Combine: $v_1=\\dfrac{m_B+m_W}{m_B}\\sqrt{2gy}$.\n5. Numbers: $v_2=\\sqrt{2(9.80)(0.0300)}=0.767$ m/s; $v_1=\\dfrac{2.005}{0.00500}(0.767)=307$ m/s.\n6. Energies: bullet before $\\tfrac12(0.00500)(307)^2=236$ J; system just after $\\tfrac12(2.005)(0.767)^2=0.590$ J. Nearly all the kinetic energy is dissipated as the wood splinters and the materials warm.","answers":["v₁ = [(m_B + m_W)/m_B]·√(2gy)","v₁ ≈ 307 m/s","v₂ ≈ 0.767 m/s just after impact","K before ≈ 236 J, K after ≈ 0.590 J"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 8.8"},{"id":"8.9","title":"Perpendicular car–truck collision","q":"A 1000-kg car traveling north at 15 m/s collides with a 2000-kg truck traveling east at 10 m/s. The occupants, wearing seat belts, are uninjured, but the two vehicles move away from the impact point as one. The insurance adjustor asks you to find the velocity of the wreckage just after impact. What is your answer? Also compare the kinetic energy before and after.","figs":["figures/ex-phys/ex8-9.webp"],"figDesc":"Before the collision: a 2000-kg truck moves along +x at 10 m/s toward the origin, and a 1000-kg car moves along +y (north) at 15 m/s toward the origin.","sol":"1. Treat car + truck as isolated during the collision (the internal forces are much larger than road friction), so the total momentum vector is conserved.\n2. Components with +x east, +y north: $P_x=m_Cv_{Cx}+m_Tv_{Tx}=(1000)(0)+(2000)(10)=2.0\\times10^4$ kg·m/s; $P_y=(1000)(15)+(2000)(0)=1.5\\times10^4$ kg·m/s.\n3. Magnitude $P=\\sqrt{(2.0\\times10^4)^2+(1.5\\times10^4)^2}=2.5\\times10^4$ kg·m/s; $\\tan\\theta=P_y/P_x=0.75$, so $\\theta=37^\\circ$ north of east.\n4. After impact $\\vec P=M\\vec V$ with $M=3000$ kg: $V=P/M=2.5\\times10^4/3000=8.3$ m/s, in the direction 37° north of east.\n5. Energy: $K_1=\\tfrac12(1000)(15)^2+\\tfrac12(2000)(10)^2=2.1\\times10^5$ J; $K_2=\\tfrac12(3000)(8.3)^2=1.0\\times10^5$ J, so about half is lost (inelastic).","answers":["V = 8.3 m/s directed 37° north of east","K before ≈ 2.1×10⁵ J, K after ≈ 1.0×10⁵ J"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 8.9"},{"id":"8.12","title":"Off-center elastic collision of two air-hockey pucks","q":"An elastic collision of two pucks on a frictionless air-hockey table. Puck A (mass 0.500 kg) has an initial velocity of 4.00 m/s in the positive x-direction and a final speed of 2.00 m/s in an unknown direction at angle α above the x-axis. Puck B (mass 0.300 kg) is initially at rest. Find the final speed v_B2 of puck B and the angles α and β (β is the direction of B\u0027s velocity below the x-axis).","figs":["figures/ex-phys/ex8-12.webp"],"figDesc":"Before the collision: puck A (m_A = 0.500 kg) moves along +x at v_A1 = 4.00 m/s toward puck B (m_B = 0.300 kg), which is at rest on the x-axis.","sol":"1. Three unknowns ($v_{B2}$, $\\alpha$, $\\beta$), three equations: energy conservation and conservation of $P_x$ and $P_y$.\n2. Energy: $\\tfrac12m_Av_{A1}^2=\\tfrac12m_Av_{A2}^2+\\tfrac12m_Bv_{B2}^2$, so $v_{B2}^2=\\dfrac{m_A(v_{A1}^2-v_{A2}^2)}{m_B}=\\dfrac{0.500(16.0-4.00)}{0.300}$, giving $v_{B2}=4.47$ m/s.\n3. x-momentum: $(0.500)(4.00)=(0.500)(2.00)\\cos\\alpha+(0.300)(4.47)\\cos\\beta$.\n4. y-momentum: $0=(0.500)(2.00)\\sin\\alpha-(0.300)(4.47)\\sin\\beta$.\n5. Isolate $\\cos\\beta$ and $\\sin\\beta$ from the two equations, square and add (using $\\sin^2\\beta+\\cos^2\\beta=1$) to eliminate $\\beta$ and solve for $\\alpha=36.9^\\circ$; then $\\beta=26.6^\\circ$.\n6. Check: $p_{A2y}=+0.600$ and $p_{B2y}=-0.600$ kg·m/s sum to zero.","answers":["v_B2 = 4.47 m/s","α = 36.9°","β = 26.6°"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 8.12"},{"id":"8.14","title":"Tug-of-war on frictionless ice","q":"James (mass 90.0 kg) and Ramon (mass 60.0 kg) are 20.0 m apart on a frozen pond. Midway between them is a mug of their favorite beverage. They pull on the ends of a light rope stretched between them. When James has moved 6.0 m toward the mug, how far and in what direction has Ramon moved? (Assume the ice is frictionless and both start at rest.)","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Frictionless horizontal surface: no net external force on James + Ramon + rope, and they start at rest, so total momentum is zero and the center of mass stays at rest.\n2. Take the origin at the mug and +x toward Ramon. Initial positions $x_J=-10.0$ m, $x_R=+10.0$ m.\n3. $x_{cm}=\\dfrac{(90.0)(-10.0)+(60.0)(10.0)}{150.0}=-2.0$ m.\n4. James moves 6.0 m toward the mug: $x_J=-4.0$ m. Let Ramon be at $x_2$: $-2.0=\\dfrac{(90.0)(-4.0)+(60.0)x_2}{150.0}$.\n5. Solving, $x_2=+1.0$ m: Ramon has moved 9.0 m toward the mug and is 1.0 m from it.\n6. The distances moved (6.0 m : 9.0 m = 2:3) are in the inverse ratio of the masses. Ramon reaches the mug first, independent of how hard anyone pulls.","answers":["Ramon moves 9.0 m toward the mug (ending at +1.0 m from it); his displacement is inversely proportional to his mass relative to James"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 8.14"},{"id":"8.16","title":"Rocket acceleration and speed in outer space","q":"The engine of a rocket in outer space, far from any planet, is turned on. The rocket ejects burned fuel at a constant rate; in the first second of firing, it ejects 1/120 of its initial mass m₀ at a relative speed (exhaust speed) of 2400 m/s. (a) What is the rocket\u0027s initial acceleration? (b) Suppose 3/4 of the initial mass of the rocket is fuel, so that the fuel is completely consumed at a constant rate in 90 s and the final mass of the rocket is m₀/4. If the rocket starts from rest, find its speed at the end of this time.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Momentum conservation for rocket plus exhaust in free space gives $m\\,dv=-v_{ex}\\,dm$, i.e. thrust $F=-v_{ex}\\,dm/dt$ and $a=-\\dfrac{v_{ex}}{m}\\dfrac{dm}{dt}$.\n2. (a) Initially $dm/dt=-m_0/(120\\text{ s})$, so $a=\\dfrac{2400\\text{ m/s}}{m_0}\\cdot\\dfrac{m_0}{120\\text{ s}}=20$ m/s², independent of $m_0$.\n3. (b) Integrate $dv=-v_{ex}\\,dm/m$: $v=v_0+v_{ex}\\ln(m_0/m)$.\n4. With $v_0=0$ and $m=m_0/4$: $v=(2400\\text{ m/s})\\ln4=3327$ m/s.\n5. Note the acceleration rises during the burn because m falls (at constant thrust, a grows as 1/m). When the rocket reaches 2400 m/s the exhaust is momentarily at rest in the starting frame; thereafter it moves forward in that frame.","answers":["(a) a = 20 m/s²","(b) v = v_ex ln 4 ≈ 3.3×10³ m/s (3327 m/s)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Examples 8.15 and 8.16"},{"id":"9.3","title":"Blu-ray disc slowing to a stop","q":"You have finished watching a movie on Blu-ray and the disc is slowing to a stop. The disc\u0027s angular velocity at t = 0 is 27.5 rad/s and its angular acceleration is a constant −10.0 rad/s². A line PQ on the disc\u0027s surface lies along the +x-axis at t = 0. (a) What is the disc\u0027s angular velocity at t = 0.300 s? (b) What angle does the line PQ make with the +x-axis at this time?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Constant α_z, so use the constant-acceleration relations with θ₀ = 0, ω₀z = 27.5 rad/s, α_z = −10.0 rad/s², t = 0.300 s.\n2. (a) ω_z = ω₀z + α_z t = 27.5 − (10.0)(0.300) = 24.5 rad/s.\n3. (b) θ = θ₀ + ω₀z t + ½α_z t² = (27.5)(0.300) + ½(−10.0)(0.300)² = 8.25 − 0.45 = 7.80 rad.\n4. Convert: 7.80 rad × (1 rev / 2π rad) = 1.24 rev = one full turn plus 0.24 rev = 0.24 × 360° = 87°.\n5. Check with ω² = ω₀² + 2αθ: (24.5² − 27.5²)/(2(−10.0)) = 7.80 rad, consistent.","answers":["ω_z = 24.5 rad/s","θ = 7.80 rad = 1.24 rev, so PQ is at 87° from the +x-axis (after one full turn)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 9.3"},{"id":"9.5","title":"Maximum propeller radius from tip-speed limit","q":"You are designing an airplane propeller that is to turn at 2400 rpm. The forward airspeed of the plane is to be 75.0 m/s, and the speed of the tips of the propeller blades through the air must not exceed 270 m/s (about 80% of the speed of sound in air). (a) What is the maximum possible propeller radius? (b) With this radius, what is the acceleration of the propeller tip?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Treat the tip as a particle. Its velocity through the air is the vector sum of the tangential velocity v_tan = ωr (in the plane of the propeller) and the plane\u0027s forward velocity (perpendicular to it), so v_tip² = v_plane² + (ωr)².\n2. Convert: ω = 2400 rev/min × (2π rad/rev)(1 min/60 s) = 251 rad/s.\n3. (a) Set v_tip = 270 m/s: r = √(v_tip² − v_plane²)/ω = √(270² − 75.0²)/251 = 259.4/251 = 1.03 m.\n4. (b) The angular speed is constant so a_tan = 0 and only the centripetal acceleration exists: a_rad = ω²r = (251)²(1.03) = 6.5 × 10⁴ m/s² ≈ 6600 g.\n5. Evaluate: the propeller must exert about 6.5 × 10⁴ N on each kilogram of tip material, which is why propellers are made of tough aluminum alloy.","answers":["r_max = 1.03 m","a_rad = 6.5 × 10⁴ m/s² (about 6600 g), directed toward the axis"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 9.5"},{"id":"9.6","title":"Moments of inertia of a three-disc machine part","q":"A machine part consists of three disks linked by lightweight struts (treat the disks as point masses at their centers and the struts as massless). (a) What is this body\u0027s moment of inertia about an axis through the center of disk A, perpendicular to the plane of the diagram? (b) What is its moment of inertia about an axis through the centers of disks B and C? (c) What is the body\u0027s kinetic energy if it rotates about the axis through A with angular speed ω = 4.0 rad/s?","figs":["figures/ex-phys/ex9-6.webp"],"figDesc":"Right triangle with disk A (0.30 kg) at the lower left, disk C (0.20 kg) at the lower right 0.40 m from A, and disk B (0.10 kg) directly above C, 0.30 m from C and 0.50 m from A. One axis passes through A perpendicular to the page; the other axis is the vertical line through B and C.","sol":"1. Treat the disks as point masses: I = Σ m_i r_i², r_i being the perpendicular distance to the axis.\n2. (a) Axis through A: disk A lies on the axis (r = 0) so only B and C contribute: I_A = (0.10 kg)(0.50 m)² + (0.20 kg)(0.40 m)² = 0.025 + 0.032 = 0.057 kg·m².\n3. (b) Axis through B and C: only A contributes (distance 0.40 m): I_BC = (0.30 kg)(0.40 m)² = 0.048 kg·m².\n4. (c) K = ½ I_A ω² = ½(0.057 kg·m²)(4.0 rad/s)² = 0.46 J.\n5. Comment: I depends on the axis; the part is easier to spin about axis BC than about A.","answers":["I_A = 0.057 kg·m²","I_BC = 0.048 kg·m²","K = 0.46 J"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 9.6"},{"id":"9.7","title":"Cable pulled off a cylinder: final spin speed","q":"We wrap a light, nonstretching cable around a solid cylinder of mass 50 kg and diameter 0.120 m, which rotates in frictionless bearings about a stationary horizontal axis. We pull the free end of the cable with a constant 9.0-N force for a distance of 2.0 m; it turns the cylinder as it unwinds without slipping. The cylinder is initially at rest. Find its final angular speed and the final speed of the cable.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Use energy: the cable is massless and does not slip, so no mechanical energy is lost to friction and only the cylinder gains kinetic energy; the force on the rim equals the applied force F.\n2. Work done: W_other = F s = (9.0 N)(2.0 m) = 18 J.\n3. Moment of inertia of a solid cylinder: I = ½MR² = ½(50 kg)(0.060 m)² = 0.090 kg·m².\n4. Work-energy: K₁ + U₁ + W_other = K₂ + U₂ gives 0 + 0 + 18 J = ½Iω², so ω = √(2W/I) = √(2(18)/0.090) = 20 rad/s.\n5. Cable speed equals rim speed: v = Rω = (0.060 m)(20 rad/s) = 1.2 m/s.\n6. If the cable had mass, some of the 18 J would go into its kinetic energy and ω would be smaller.","answers":["ω = 20 rad/s","cable speed v = 1.2 m/s"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 9.7"},{"id":"9.8","title":"Falling block unwinding a cylinder","q":"We wrap a light, nonstretching cable around a solid cylinder with mass M and radius R. The cylinder rotates with negligible friction about a stationary horizontal axis. We tie the free end of the cable to a block of mass m and release the block from rest at a distance h above the floor. As the block falls, the cable unwinds without stretching or slipping. Find expressions for the speed of the falling block and the angular speed of the cylinder as the block strikes the floor.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Massless, non-slipping cable: it does no net work and friction does none, so mechanical energy is conserved. Only gravity does work.\n2. Take U = 0 at floor level. Initial: K₁ = 0, U₁ = mgh. Final: block and cylinder both move, K₂ = ½mv² + ½Iω², U₂ = 0.\n3. Cylinder: I = ½MR². No slipping: v = Rω, so ω = v/R.\n4. Energy: mgh = ½mv² + ½(½MR²)(v/R)² = ½(m + ½M)v².\n5. Solve: v = √[2gh/(1 + M/2m)].\n6. Angular speed: ω = v/R = (1/R)√[2gh/(1 + M/2m)].\n7. Checks: M ≫ m gives v → 0; M ≪ m gives v → √(2gh), the free-fall speed.","answers":["v = √[2gh/(1 + M/2m)]","ω = v/R = (1/R)√[2gh/(1 + M/2m)]"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 9.8"},{"id":"9.10","title":"Moment of inertia of a hollow cylinder by integration","q":"A hollow cylinder of uniform mass density ρ has length L, inner radius R₁ and outer radius R₂ (it might be a steel cylinder in a printing press). Using integration, find its moment of inertia about its axis of symmetry, and express it in terms of its total mass M. Show that the result reduces to the solid-cylinder and thin-walled-cylinder values.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Choose a thin cylindrical shell of radius r, thickness dr and length L as the mass element; all of it is at distance r from the axis. Its volume is (2πr)(L)(dr), so dm = ρ(2πrL dr).\n2. I = ∫ r² dm = 2πρL ∫ from R₁ to R₂ of r³ dr = (2πρL/4)(R₂⁴ − R₁⁴) = (πρL/2)(R₂² − R₁²)(R₂² + R₁²).\n3. Total mass: M = ρV = πLρ(R₂² − R₁²).\n4. Substitute: I = ½ M (R₁² + R₂²).\n5. Limits: solid cylinder R₁ = 0, R₂ = R gives I = ½MR². Thin wall R₁ ≈ R₂ ≈ R gives I = MR², which also follows directly because all the mass is at the same distance R.","answers":["I = (πρL/2)(R₂⁴ − R₁⁴) = ½M(R₁² + R₂²)","solid cylinder ½MR²; thin-walled cylinder MR²"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 9.10"},{"id":"9.11","title":"Moment of inertia of a uniform solid sphere","q":"Find the moment of inertia of a solid sphere of uniform mass density ρ and radius R (like a billiard ball) about an axis through its center. You may use the known moment of inertia of a uniform disk about its axis.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Slice the sphere into thin disks of thickness dx perpendicular to the axis, at distance x from the center. Disk radius r = √(R² − x²).\n2. Volume dV = πr² dx = π(R² − x²)dx, mass dm = ρπ(R² − x²)dx.\n3. A disk has dI = ½ r² dm = ½(R² − x²)·ρπ(R² − x²)dx = (πρ/2)(R² − x²)² dx.\n4. Integrate over the whole sphere (x from −R to R, i.e. twice the right hemisphere): I = 2(πρ/2)∫ from 0 to R of (R² − x²)² dx = πρ(R⁵ − 2R⁵/3 + R⁵/5) = 8πρR⁵/15.\n5. With M = ρ(4πR³/3), ρ = 3M/(4πR³), so I = (8πR⁵/15)(3M/4πR³) = (2/5)MR².\n6. This is less than ½MR² for a solid cylinder of the same M and R, because more of the sphere\u0027s mass is near the axis.","answers":["I = 8πρR⁵/15 = (2/5)MR²"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 9.11"},{"id":"10.1","title":"Plumber standing on a wrench extension","q":"To loosen a pipe fitting, a weekend plumber slips a piece of scrap pipe (a \"cheater\") over his wrench handle. He stands on the end of the cheater, applying his full 900-N weight at a point 0.80 m from the center of the fitting. The wrench handle and cheater make an angle of 19° with the horizontal. Find the magnitude and direction of the torque he applies about the center of the fitting.","figs":["figures/ex-phys/ex10-1.webp"],"figDesc":"Side view: a wrench on a pipe fitting at the right, with the handle and cheater sloping up to the left at 19° above the horizontal. A person\u0027s foot at the upper-left end applies a downward force F = 900 N, at a distance 0.80 m measured along the handle from the fitting center.","sol":"1. The force is vertical (down), the position vector r (0.80 m) runs from the fitting center along the handle to the foot. The angle between r and F is φ = 109° (equivalently 71° with sin 71° = sin 109°).\n2. Lever arm: l = r sin φ = (0.80 m) sin 109° = 0.76 m.\n3. Magnitude: τ = Fl = (900 N)(0.76 m) = 680 N·m. Check with τ = rF sin φ = (0.80)(900)(sin 109°) = 680 N·m, or with the tangential component F_tan = F cos 19° = 851 N: τ = F_tan r = (851)(0.80) = 680 N·m.\n4. Direction: τ = r × F. Curling the fingers of the right hand from r (up and to the left along the handle) to F (straight down) puts the thumb out of the page. This is the counterclockwise sense about the fitting.","answers":["τ = 680 N·m","directed out of the page of the diagram, i.e. a counterclockwise torque about the fitting"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 10.1"},{"id":"10.3","title":"Block falling while unwinding a cylinder: acceleration and tension","q":"A light, nonstretching cable is wrapped around a solid cylinder of mass M and radius R. The cylinder rotates with negligible friction about a stationary horizontal axis. The free end of the cable is tied to a block of mass m, which is released from rest. The cable unwinds without stretching or slipping. Find the acceleration of the falling block and the tension in the cable.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Two bodies: block (translation) and cylinder (rotation about its fixed axis). Take downward positive for the block and counterclockwise (the sense produced by the cable) positive for the cylinder.\n2. Block: mg − T = m a_y.\n3. Cylinder: the only torque about the axis is from the tension: TR = I α_z = (½MR²) α_z, so T = ½ M R α_z.\n4. No slipping: a_y = R α_z, hence T = ½ M a_y.\n5. Substitute into the block equation: mg − ½ M a_y = m a_y, so a_y = g/(1 + M/2m).\n6. Tension: T = ½ M a_y = mg/(1 + 2m/M).\n7. Checks: M → 0 gives a_y = g, T = 0 (free fall); M ≫ m gives a_y ≪ g and T ≈ mg. The acceleration is less than g and T \u003c mg, as it must be. Falling h from rest gives v = √(2gh/(1 + M/2m)), matching the energy result.","answers":["a_y = g/(1 + M/2m)","T = mg/(1 + 2m/M) = ½ M a_y"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 10.3"},{"id":"10.6","title":"Primitive yo-yo: acceleration and string tension","q":"You make a primitive yo-yo by wrapping a massless string around a solid cylinder of mass M and radius R. You hold the free end of the string stationary and release the cylinder from rest. The string unwinds without slipping or stretching as the cylinder descends and rotates. Find the downward acceleration of the cylinder and the tension in the string.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. The cylinder\u0027s center of mass translates (down) while the cylinder rotates about its symmetry axis, which keeps a fixed direction, so use ΣF = M a_cm and Στ_cm = I_cm α with I_cm = ½MR².\n2. Translation (down positive): Mg − T = M a_cm,y.\n3. Rotation: only T gives torque about the axis: TR = I_cm α_z = ½MR² α_z.\n4. No slipping at the string: v_cm = Rω, so a_cm,y = R α_z.\n5. Eliminate α_z: T = ½ M a_cm,y.\n6. Then Mg − ½ M a_cm,y = M a_cm,y, so a_cm,y = 2g/3.\n7. T = ½ M (2g/3) = Mg/3.\n8. The result is less than g and the tension is less than the weight, consistent with the energy result v_cm = √(4gh/3) after falling h.","answers":["a_cm = 2g/3 (downward)","T = Mg/3"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 10.6"},{"id":"10.7","title":"Bowling ball rolling down a ramp: acceleration and friction","q":"A bowling ball rolls without slipping down a ramp inclined at an angle β to the horizontal. What are the ball\u0027s acceleration and the magnitude of the friction force on the ball? Treat the ball as a uniform solid sphere of mass M and radius R, ignoring the finger holes.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Take down the slope as positive x. Forces: weight component Mg sin β down the slope, static friction f up the slope at the contact point, normal force. Only f gives a torque about the center of mass.\n2. Translation: Mg sin β − f = M a_cm,x.\n3. Rotation: fR = I_cm α_z = (2/5)MR² α_z.\n4. No slipping: a_cm,x = R α_z, so f = (2/5) M a_cm,x.\n5. Substitute: Mg sin β = M a_cm,x (1 + 2/5), hence a_cm,x = (5/7) g sin β.\n6. Friction: f = (2/5) M (5/7) g sin β = (2/7) M g sin β.\n7. Static friction must be at least this large: f ≤ μ_s n = μ_s Mg cos β, so rolling without slipping needs μ_s ≥ (2/7) tan β. Comparing with sliding on a frictionless ramp (a = g sin β), rolling is slower because part of the energy goes into rotation.","answers":["a_cm = (5/7) g sin β, down the slope","f = (2/7) M g sin β, directed up the slope (static friction)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 10.7"},{"id":"10.8","title":"Motor torque on a grindstone: work, energy and power","q":"An electric motor exerts a constant torque of 10 N·m on a grindstone, which has a moment of inertia of 2.0 kg·m² about its shaft. The system starts from rest. Find the work W done by the motor in 8.0 s, the grindstone\u0027s kinetic energy K at this time, and the average power delivered by the motor.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Constant net torque, so constant angular acceleration: α_z = τ_z/I = (10 N·m)/(2.0 kg·m²) = 5.0 rad/s².\n2. After t = 8.0 s: ω_z = α_z t = 40 rad/s and Δθ = ½ α_z t² = ½(5.0)(8.0)² = 160 rad.\n3. Work: W = τ_z Δθ = (10 N·m)(160 rad) = 1600 J.\n4. Kinetic energy: K = ½ I ω² = ½(2.0 kg·m²)(40 rad/s)² = 1600 J, equal to W as required by the work-energy theorem (initial K = 0).\n5. Average power: P_av = W/t = 1600 J / 8.0 s = 200 W.\n6. Check: instantaneous power P = τ_z ω_z grows linearly from 0 to (10)(40) = 400 W at t = 8.0 s, so its average is 200 W.","answers":["W = 1600 J","K = 1600 J","P_av = 200 W"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 10.8"},{"id":"10.10","title":"Professor with dumbbells on a turntable","q":"A physics professor stands at the center of a frictionless turntable with arms outstretched and a 5.0-kg dumbbell in each hand. He is set rotating about the vertical axis, making one revolution in 2.0 s. Find his final angular velocity if he pulls the dumbbells in to his stomach. His moment of inertia (without the dumbbells) is 3.0 kg·m² with arms outstretched and 2.2 kg·m² with his hands at his stomach. The dumbbells are 1.0 m from the axis initially and 0.20 m at the end. Also find the initial and final kinetic energies.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. No external torque about the vertical axis, so L_z = Iω is conserved. Treat each dumbbell as a particle contributing m r² to I.\n2. Initial: I₁ = 3.0 + 2(5.0)(1.0)² = 13 kg·m².\n3. Final: I₂ = 2.2 + 2(5.0)(0.20)² = 2.6 kg·m².\n4. ω₁ = 0.50 rev/s = 3.14 rad/s. Conservation: ω₂ = (I₁/I₂) ω₁ = (13/2.6)(0.50 rev/s) = 2.5 rev/s = 15.7 rad/s (the revolution units cancel in the ratio).\n5. Kinetic energies: K₁ = ½ I₁ ω₁² = ½(13)(3.14)² = 64 J; K₂ = ½ I₂ ω₂² = ½(2.6)(15.7)² = 320 J.\n6. The fivefold increase in K equals the work he does pulling the arms and dumbbells inward against the \u0027centrifugal\u0027 tendency.","answers":["ω₂ = 2.5 rev/s = 15.7 rad/s (five times the initial rate)","K₁ = 64 J, K₂ = 320 J"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 10.10"},{"id":"10.12","title":"Bullet embedding in a swinging door","q":"A door 1.00 m wide, of mass 15 kg, can rotate freely about a vertical axis through its hinges. A bullet with a mass of 10 g and a speed of 400 m/s strikes the center of the door, in a direction perpendicular to the plane of the door, and embeds itself there. Find the door\u0027s angular speed. Is kinetic energy conserved?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. System: door plus bullet. No external torque about the hinge axis during the impact (hinge forces have zero lever arm), so angular momentum about the hinge axis is conserved.\n2. Initial: L = m v l = (0.010 kg)(400 m/s)(0.50 m) = 2.0 kg·m²/s.\n3. Final: L = Iω with I = I_door + I_bullet. Door about its edge: I_door = M d²/3 = (15)(1.00)²/3 = 5.0 kg·m². Bullet: I_bullet = m l² = (0.010)(0.50)² = 0.0025 kg·m². So I = 5.0025 kg·m².\n4. ω = m v l / I = 2.0/5.0025 = 0.40 rad/s.\n5. Energy: K₁ = ½ m v² = ½(0.010)(400)² = 800 J; K₂ = ½ I ω² = ½(5.0025)(0.40)² = 0.40 J.\n6. Only 1/2000 of the kinetic energy remains: the collision is inelastic (friction while the bullet embeds), so kinetic energy is not conserved. At 0.40 rad/s the door needs about 3.9 s to swing 90°.","answers":["ω = 0.40 rad/s","No: K falls from 800 J to 0.40 J (1/2000 of the initial value)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 10.12"},{"id":"11.1","title":"Plank on two sawhorses with a cousin at the end","q":"A uniform plank of length L = 6.0 m and mass M = 90 kg rests on two sawhorses separated by D = 1.5 m and equidistant from the center of the plank. Cousin Throckmorton wants to stand on the right-hand end of the plank. If the plank is to remain at rest, how massive can Throckmorton be?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. At the limit of balance the plank is about to tip about the right-hand sawhorse, so the center of gravity of plank + person lies directly above that sawhorse (the left support force then vanishes).\n2. Origin at the plank\u0027s center C (its cg). The plank\u0027s cg is at x = 0, Throckmorton at x = L/2 = 3.0 m, the right-hand sawhorse at x = D/2 = 0.75 m.\n3. x_cg = [M(0) + m(L/2)] / (M + m) = D/2.\n4. Solve: m(L/2) = (D/2)(M + m), so m = M(D/2) / [(L/2) − (D/2)] = (90 kg)(0.75 m)/(2.25 m) = 30 kg.\n5. Check with the origin at the right-hand sawhorse: M(−D/2) + m(L/2 − D/2) = 0 gives the same m = 30 kg; the result does not depend on the origin. (A 60-kg person could stand only halfway between the sawhorse and the end.)","answers":["m = 30 kg (maximum mass for the plank to stay at rest)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 11.1"},{"id":"11.3","title":"Climber on a ladder against a frictionless wall","q":"Sir Lancelot, who weighs 800 N, is assaulting a castle by climbing a uniform ladder that is 5.0 m long and weighs 180 N. The bottom of the ladder rests on a ledge and leans across the moat in equilibrium against a frictionless, vertical castle wall. The ladder makes an angle of 53.1° with the horizontal. Lancelot pauses one-third of the way up the ladder. (a) Find the normal and friction forces on the base of the ladder. (b) Find the minimum coefficient of static friction needed to prevent slipping at the base. (c) Find the magnitude and direction of the contact force on the base of the ladder.","figs":["figures/ex-phys/ex11-3.webp"],"figDesc":"A knight climbing a uniform ladder that leans against a vertical wall labeled \u0027Frictionless wall\u0027; the ladder\u0027s base rests on a ledge and the ladder makes an angle of 53.1° with the horizontal.","sol":"1. Ladder + Lancelot are in equilibrium. Forces: wall normal force n₁ (horizontal, at the top, frictionless), floor normal force n₂ (up) and static friction f_s (horizontal) at the base B, weights 180 N (ladder cg, at mid-length) and 800 N (at one-third up).\n2. ΣF_x = f_s − n₁ = 0 and ΣF_y = n₂ − 800 − 180 = 0, so n₂ = 980 N.\n3. Torques about B (removes n₂ and f_s). Using the 3-4-5 triangle (53.1°): lever arms 1.5 m for the ladder weight, 1.0 m for Lancelot, 4.0 m for n₁. Στ_B = n₁(4.0) − 180(1.5) − 800(1.0) = 0, so n₁ = 268 N.\n4. Then f_s = n₁ = 268 N.\n5. (b) f_s ≤ μ_s n₂, so (μ_s)_min = 268/980 = 0.27.\n6. (c) Contact force at the base F_B = f_s î + n₂ ĵ = (268 N)î + (980 N)ĵ; magnitude √(268² + 980²) = 1020 N, directed at θ = arctan(980/268) = 75° above the horizontal, not along the ladder.\n7. The required friction grows as Lancelot climbs; a steeper ladder (e.g. 75°) reduces it.","answers":["n₂ = 980 N (normal at base), f_s = 268 N (and wall force n₁ = 268 N)","(μ_s)_min = 0.27","F_B = 1020 N at 75° above the horizontal"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 11.3"},{"id":"11.4","title":"Forearm, biceps tendon and dumbbell in equilibrium","q":"A horizontal human forearm holds a dumbbell of weight w = 200 N in the hand. The forearm is in equilibrium under the weight w, the tension T in the tendon of the biceps muscle, and the force E exerted on the forearm by the upper arm at the elbow joint. The tendon is attached at a point a distance D = 0.050 m from the elbow and pulls at an angle θ = 80° to the horizontal; the dumbbell is a horizontal distance L = 0.30 m from the elbow. Neglect the weight of the forearm. Find T and the horizontal and vertical components of E.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Take torques about the elbow so that E does not appear. Only the vertical component of T (T_y = T sin θ, at lever arm D) and the weight (lever arm L) act: Στ_elbow = Lw − D T_y = 0, so T_y = Lw/D and T = Lw/(D sin θ) = (0.30)(200)/[(0.050)(0.985)] = 1220 N.\n2. Horizontal: T_x = T cos θ (toward the shoulder side) is balanced by the elbow force: ΣF_x = T_x − E_x = 0, so E_x = T cos θ = 210 N.\n3. Vertical: ΣF_y = T_y + E_y − w = 0 with T_y = Lw/D = 1200 N, so E_y = w − Lw/D = −(L − D)w/D = −1000 N (the minus sign: E_y points downward).\n4. Magnitude at the elbow: E = √(E_x² + E_y²) = √(210² + 1000²) ≈ 1020 N.\n5. Check by torques about the tendon attachment A: (L − D)w + D E_y = 0 gives the same E_y.\n6. Evaluate: T and E are 5 to 6 times the 200-N load, because the biceps works at a very short lever arm, so neglecting the forearm\u0027s own 20 N is reasonable.","answers":["T ≈ 1220 N","E_x ≈ 210 N (horizontal)","E_y = −1000 N (downward)","|E| ≈ 1020 N"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 11.4"},{"id":"11.5","title":"Steel rod carrying a milling machine","q":"A steel rod 2.0 m long has a cross-sectional area of 0.30 cm². It is hung by one end from a support, and a 550-kg milling machine is hung from its other end. Determine the stress on the rod and the resulting strain and elongation. (Young\u0027s modulus of steel Y = 2.0 × 10¹¹ Pa.)","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. The rod is in tension; the force perpendicular to the cross section is the machine\u0027s weight F = mg = (550 kg)(9.8 m/s²) = 5.4 × 10³ N.\n2. Tensile stress = F/A = (5.4 × 10³ N)/(0.30 × 10⁻⁴ m²) = 1.8 × 10⁸ Pa.\n3. Strain from Hooke\u0027s law: strain = stress/Y = (1.8 × 10⁸ Pa)/(2.0 × 10¹¹ Pa) = 9.0 × 10⁻⁴.\n4. Elongation: Δl = (strain)(l₀) = (9.0 × 10⁻⁴)(2.0 m) = 1.8 × 10⁻³ m = 1.8 mm.\n5. Evaluate: only 1.8 mm for a load of over half a ton shows the stiffness of steel.","answers":["stress = 1.8 × 10⁸ Pa","strain = 9.0 × 10⁻⁴","elongation = 1.8 mm"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 11.5"},{"id":"11.6","title":"Oil in a hydraulic press under pressure","q":"A hydraulic press contains 0.25 m³ (250 L) of oil. Find the decrease in the volume of the oil when it is subjected to a pressure increase Δp = 1.6 × 10⁷ Pa (about 160 atm or 2300 psi). The bulk modulus of the oil is B = 5.0 × 10⁹ Pa (about 5.0 × 10⁴ atm) and its compressibility is k = 1/B = 20 × 10⁻⁶ atm⁻¹. Also state the fractional change in volume.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Bulk modulus definition: B = −Δp/(ΔV/V₀), so ΔV = −V₀ Δp / B.\n2. ΔV = −(0.25 m³)(1.6 × 10⁷ Pa)/(5.0 × 10⁹ Pa) = −8.0 × 10⁻⁴ m³ = −0.80 L.\n3. Alternative with the compressibility: ΔV = −k V₀ Δp = −(20 × 10⁻⁶ atm⁻¹)(0.25 m³)(160 atm) = −8.0 × 10⁻⁴ m³.\n4. Fractional change: ΔV/V₀ = −8.0 × 10⁻⁴/0.25 = −3.2 × 10⁻³, i.e. −0.32%.\n5. The sign is negative: volume decreases when pressure increases. Even for 160 atm the fractional change is tiny.","answers":["ΔV = −8.0 × 10⁻⁴ m³ = −0.80 L","ΔV/V₀ = −0.0032 (−0.32%)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 11.6"},{"id":"11.7","title":"Brass base plate sheared in an earthquake","q":"The brass base plate of an outdoor sculpture experiences shear forces in an earthquake. The plate is 0.80 m square and 0.50 cm thick. What is the force exerted on each of its edges if the resulting displacement x of one face relative to the opposite face is 0.16 mm? (Shear modulus of brass S = 3.5 × 10¹⁰ Pa.)","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Shear strain = x/h, with h the transverse dimension of the plate across which the displacement occurs: h = 0.80 m. Strain = (0.16 × 10⁻³ m)/(0.80 m) = 2.0 × 10⁻⁴.\n2. Shear stress = S × strain = (3.5 × 10¹⁰ Pa)(2.0 × 10⁻⁴) = 7.0 × 10⁶ Pa.\n3. Shear stress = F_∥/A, with A the area of an edge face on which the force acts: A = (0.80 m)(0.0050 m) = 4.0 × 10⁻³ m².\n4. F_∥ = (7.0 × 10⁶ Pa)(4.0 × 10⁻³ m²) = 2.8 × 10⁴ N.\n5. The force is about 28 kN, tangent to each edge, and the plate\u0027s thinness makes the area small.","answers":["shear strain = 2.0 × 10⁻⁴","shear stress = 7.0 × 10⁶ Pa","F = 2.8 × 10⁴ N on each edge"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 11.7"},{"id":"12.4","title":"Oil and water in an open U-tube","q":"A manometer (U-shaped) tube is partially filled with water. Oil, which does not mix with water, is poured into the left arm until the oil–water interface is at the midpoint of the tube, as shown. Both arms of the tube are open to the air. Find a relationship between the heights h_oil and h_water, where h_oil is the height of the oil surface above the interface and h_water is the height of the water surface in the right arm above that same interface level. Express it using the densities ρ_oil and ρ_water.","figs":[],"figDesc":"U-tube open at both ends; oil in the left arm resting on water, the oil–water interface at the tube\u0027s midpoint level; the water surface in the right arm is lower than the oil surface in the left arm.","sol":"1. Approach: p = p₀ + ρgh holds only within a fluid of uniform density, so write it separately for each fluid column down to the interface level.\n2. Both free surfaces are at atmospheric pressure p₀; the pressure p at the interface level is the same in both arms (same level, connected fluid).\n3. Left arm: $p=p_0+\\rho_{oil}\\,g\\,h_{oil}$. Right arm: $p=p_0+\\rho_{water}\\,g\\,h_{water}$.\n4. Equate: $\\rho_{oil}h_{oil}=\\rho_{water}h_{water}$, so $h_{oil}=\\dfrac{\\rho_{water}}{\\rho_{oil}}\\,h_{water}$.\n5. Check: water is denser than oil (oil about 850 kg/m³ vs 1000), so h_oil \u003e h_water; a taller column of the lighter fluid gives the same bottom pressure.","answers":["h_oil = (ρ_water/ρ_oil)·h_water, i.e. ρ_oil·h_oil = ρ_water·h_water","The lighter fluid (oil) stands higher"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 12.4"},{"id":"12.5","title":"Hoisting a gold statue from the sea","q":"A 15.0 kg solid gold statue is raised from the sea bottom. What is the tension in the hoisting cable (assumed massless) when the statue is (a) at rest and completely underwater, and (b) at rest and completely out of the water? Use density of gold 19.3 × 10³ kg/m³, seawater 1.03 × 10³ kg/m³, air 1.2 kg/m³, g = 9.80 m/s².","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: the statue is in equilibrium under weight mg, cable tension T and buoyant force B (weight of the displaced fluid, Archimedes).\n2. Volume: $V=m/\\rho_{gold}=15.0/(19.3\\times10^3)=7.77\\times10^{-4}$ m³.\n3. (a) $B_{sw}=\\rho_{sw}Vg=(1.03\\times10^3)(7.77\\times10^{-4})(9.80)=7.84$ N.\n4. Equilibrium: $B+T-mg=0$, so $T_{sw}=mg-B_{sw}=147\\ \\text{N}-7.84\\ \\text{N}=139$ N.\n5. (b) In air: $B_{air}=(1.2)(7.77\\times10^{-4})(9.80)=9.1\\times10^{-3}$ N, negligible, so $T_{air}=mg=147$ N.\n6. Note that B depends on the fluid density, not the statue\u0027s; if the fluid were as dense as gold, T would be zero.","answers":["(a) T = 139 N (buoyant force 7.84 N)","(b) T = 147 N (buoyancy in air negligible)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 12.5"},{"id":"12.6","title":"Oil pumped through a narrowing pipe","q":"Incompressible oil of density 850 kg/m³ is pumped through a cylindrical pipe at a rate of 9.5 liters per second. (a) The first section of the pipe has a diameter of 8.0 cm. What is the flow speed of the oil? What is the mass flow rate? (b) The second section of the pipe has a diameter of 4.0 cm. What are the flow speed and mass flow rate in that section?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: incompressible flow, so the volume flow rate dV/dt = Av is the same in every section, and so is the mass flow rate ρ·dV/dt.\n2. (a) $v_1=\\dfrac{dV/dt}{A_1}=\\dfrac{9.5\\times10^{-3}\\ \\text{m}^3/\\text{s}}{\\pi(4.0\\times10^{-2}\\ \\text{m})^2}=1.9$ m/s.\n3. Mass flow rate: $\\rho\\,dV/dt=(850)(9.5\\times10^{-3})=8.1$ kg/s.\n4. (b) Continuity: $v_2=\\dfrac{A_1}{A_2}v_1=\\dfrac{(4.0)^2}{(2.0)^2}(1.9)=7.6$ m/s = 4v₁.\n5. Volume and mass flow rates are unchanged (9.5 L/s and 8.1 kg/s).\n6. Check: half the diameter means one quarter of the area, hence four times the speed.","answers":["(a) v₁ = 1.9 m/s; mass flow rate = 8.1 kg/s","(b) v₂ = 7.6 m/s; same volume (9.5 L/s) and mass (8.1 kg/s) flow rates"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 12.6"},{"id":"12.7","title":"Water pressure reaching the second floor","q":"Water enters a house through a pipe with an inside diameter of 2.0 cm at an absolute pressure of 4.0 × 10⁵ Pa (about 4 atm). A 1.0-cm-diameter pipe leads to the second-floor bathroom 5.0 m above. When the flow speed at the inlet pipe is 1.5 m/s, find the flow speed, pressure and volume flow rate in the bathroom. Take ρ = 1.0 × 10³ kg/m³ and g = 9.8 m/s². Ignore internal friction.","figs":["figures/ex-phys/ex12-23.webp"],"figDesc":"Cutaway of a house: water supply enters through a 2-cm pipe at point 1 in the basement (past a water meter); a 1-cm pipe rises 5.0 m to point 2 at the second-floor bathroom, with a hot-water tank beside the riser.","sol":"1. Approach: steady, incompressible flow, negligible viscosity in a wide pipe: use continuity for the speed and Bernoulli for the pressure. Point 1 at the inlet (y₁ = 0), point 2 at the bathroom (y₂ = 5.0 m).\n2. Continuity: $v_2=\\dfrac{A_1}{A_2}v_1=\\dfrac{\\pi(1.0\\ \\text{cm})^2}{\\pi(0.50\\ \\text{cm})^2}(1.5)=6.0$ m/s.\n3. Bernoulli: $p_2=p_1-\\tfrac12\\rho(v_2^2-v_1^2)-\\rho g(y_2-y_1)$.\n4. $p_2=4.0\\times10^5-\\tfrac12(1.0\\times10^3)(36-2.25)-(1.0\\times10^3)(9.8)(5.0)=4.0\\times10^5-0.17\\times10^5-0.49\\times10^5=3.3\\times10^5$ Pa (3.3 atm).\n5. Volume flow rate: $A_2v_2=\\pi(0.50\\times10^{-2})^2(6.0)=4.7\\times10^{-4}$ m³/s = 0.47 L/s.\n6. Check: if the tap is closed, v₁ = v₂ = 0 and p₂ rises to 3.5 × 10⁵ Pa.","answers":["v₂ = 6.0 m/s","p₂ = 3.3 × 10⁵ Pa (about 3.3 atm)","dV/dt = 4.7 × 10⁻⁴ m³/s = 0.47 L/s"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 12.7"},{"id":"12.8","title":"Speed of efflux from a storage tank","q":"A gasoline storage tank has cross-sectional area A₁ and is filled to a depth h. The space above the gasoline contains air at pressure p₀, and the gasoline flows out the bottom through a short pipe of cross-sectional area A₂ at atmospheric pressure p_atm. Derive expressions for the flow speed in the pipe and the volume flow rate. Assume A₁ is very much larger than A₂ and ignore viscosity.","figs":["figures/ex-phys/ex12-24.webp"],"figDesc":"Cylindrical storage tank with liquid depth h above the exit pipe; point 1 at the liquid surface (area A₁, air pressure p₀ above), point 2 in the exit pipe at the bottom (area A₂, outside pressure p_atm).","sol":"1. Approach: treat the moving liquid as one flow tube of incompressible, frictionless fluid and apply Bernoulli between the surface (1) and the exit (2). Take y = 0 at the exit, so y₁ = h, y₂ = 0.\n2. Since A₁ ≫ A₂, the surface drops very slowly: v₁ ≈ 0 (continuity gives v₁ = (A₂/A₁)v₂).\n3. Bernoulli: $p_0+\\tfrac12\\rho v_1^2+\\rho g h=p_{atm}+\\tfrac12\\rho v_2^2+0$.\n4. Solve with v₁ = 0: $v_2=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2(p_0-p_{atm})}{\\rho}+2gh}$.\n5. Volume flow rate: $dV/dt=v_2A_2$.\n6. Limit: if the tank is vented, p₀ = p_atm and $v_2=\\sqrt{2gh}$ (Torricelli\u0027s theorem), the speed of free fall through h; this also applies to a side hole at depth h, giving dV/dt = A₂√(2gh).","answers":["v₂ = √[2(p₀ − p_atm)/ρ + 2gh]","dV/dt = A₂ v₂","Vented tank: v₂ = √(2gh) (Torricelli)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 12.8"},{"id":"12.9","title":"Flow speed from a Venturi meter","q":"A Venturi meter measures flow speed in a pipe. The wide part (point 1) has cross-sectional area A₁ and the narrow throat (point 2) has area A₂. Two vertical tubes open into the pipe at points 1 and 2, and the liquid stands higher in the tube at point 1 by a height difference h. Derive an expression for the flow speed v₁ in terms of A₁, A₂, h and g. Assume steady, incompressible flow with negligible internal friction.","figs":["figures/ex-phys/ex12-25.webp"],"figDesc":"Horizontal pipe with a wide section (area A₁, speed v₁, pressure p₁) narrowing to a throat (area A₂, speed v₂, pressure p₂); two vertical open tubes at the two sections show liquid levels differing by a height h.","sol":"1. Approach: Bernoulli between points 1 and 2 at the same height (y₁ = y₂), plus continuity; the manometer tubes relate h to p₁ − p₂ via p = p₀ + ρgh.\n2. Bernoulli: $p_1+\\tfrac12\\rho v_1^2=p_2+\\tfrac12\\rho v_2^2$.\n3. Continuity: $v_2=(A_1/A_2)\\,v_1$.\n4. Therefore $p_1-p_2=\\tfrac12\\rho v_1^2\\left[\\left(\\dfrac{A_1}{A_2}\\right)^2-1\\right]$.\n5. Hydrostatics: $p_1-p_2=\\rho g h$.\n6. Equate and solve: $v_1=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2gh}{(A_1/A_2)^2-1}}$.\n7. Check: the throat is faster (v₂ \u003e v₁) so p₂ \u003c p₁, consistent with the lower column at point 2; as A₂ → A₁ the denominator tends to zero, so a measurable h would need a very large speed: a weak constriction is a poor meter.","answers":["v₁ = √[2gh / ((A₁/A₂)² − 1)]","p₁ − p₂ = ρgh"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 12.9"},{"id":"13.4","title":"Weight of a lander on Mars","q":"A robotic lander with an earth weight of 3430 N is sent to Mars, which has radius R_M = 3.40 × 10⁶ m and mass m_M = 6.42 × 10²³ kg. Find the weight F_g of the lander on the Martian surface and the acceleration due to gravity g_M there.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. The lander\u0027s mass is the same everywhere: m = w/g = (3430 N)/(9.80 m/s²) = 350 kg.\n2. Weight on Mars: F_g = G m_M m / R_M² = (6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N·m²/kg²)(6.42 × 10²³ kg)(350 kg)/(3.40 × 10⁶ m)² = 1.30 × 10³ N.\n3. Acceleration: g_M = F_g/m = (1.30 × 10³ N)/(350 kg) = 3.7 m/s² (or directly g_M = G m_M/R_M²).\n4. Check: 3.7/9.8 = 0.38, the weight on Mars is about 38% of the earth weight.","answers":["F_g ≈ 1.30 × 10³ N","g_M ≈ 3.7 m/s²"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 13.4"},{"id":"13.5","title":"Shell shot upward from the earth: muzzle speed","q":"In Jules Verne\u0027s 1865 story \"From the Earth to the Moon,\" three men went to the moon in a shell fired from a giant cannon sunk in the earth in Florida. (a) Find the minimum muzzle speed needed to shoot a shell straight up to a height above the earth equal to the earth\u0027s radius R_E. (b) Find the minimum muzzle speed that would allow a shell to escape from the earth completely (the escape speed). Neglect air resistance, the earth\u0027s rotation, and the gravitational pull of the moon. The earth\u0027s radius and mass are R_E = 6.38 × 10⁶ m and m_E = 5.97 × 10²⁴ kg.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Only gravity does work after launch, so K₁ + U₁ = K₂ + U₂ with U = −G m_E m/r.\n2. Point 1 is at r₁ = R_E with speed v₁ (target); at the highest point K₂ = 0.\n3. (a) r₂ = 2R_E: ½ m v₁² − G m_E m/R_E = −G m_E m/(2R_E), so v₁ = √(G m_E/R_E) = √[(6.67 × 10⁻¹¹)(5.97 × 10²⁴)/(6.38 × 10⁶)] = 7.90 × 10³ m/s.\n4. (b) r₂ = ∞, U₂ = 0 and K₂ = 0 (minimum): ½ m v₁² = G m_E m/R_E, so v₁ = √(2 G m_E/R_E) = 1.12 × 10⁴ m/s.\n5. The result does not depend on the shell\u0027s mass. This is the escape speed (11.2 km/s), √2 times the part (a) result; the minimum total energy to reach infinity is zero.","answers":["(a) v₁ = √(Gm_E/R_E) = 7.90 × 10³ m/s","(b) v_esc = √(2Gm_E/R_E) = 1.12 × 10⁴ m/s"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 13.5"},{"id":"13.6","title":"Putting a 1000-kg satellite into a 300-km orbit","q":"You wish to put a 1000-kg satellite into a circular orbit 300 km above the earth\u0027s surface. (a) What speed, period, and radial acceleration will it have? (b) How much work must be done to the satellite to put it in orbit? (c) How much additional work would have to be done to make the satellite escape the earth? Use R_E = 6.38 × 10⁶ m and m_E = 5.97 × 10²⁴ kg and ignore the earth\u0027s rotation.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Orbit radius r = R_E + 300 km = 6.68 × 10⁶ m.\n2. (a) Gravity supplies the centripetal force: v = √(G m_E/r) = √[(6.67 × 10⁻¹¹)(5.97 × 10²⁴)/(6.68 × 10⁶)] = 7.73 × 10³ m/s.\n3. Period T = 2πr/v = 2π(6.68 × 10⁶)/(7.73 × 10³) = 5.43 × 10³ s ≈ 90.5 min.\n4. Radial acceleration a_rad = v²/r = 8.93 m/s² (this is g at 300 km, about 10% less than at the surface).\n5. (b) Energy in orbit E₂ = −G m_E m/(2r) = −2.98 × 10¹⁰ J. At the launch pad (K = 0): E₁ = −G m_E m/R_E = −6.25 × 10¹⁰ J. Work W = E₂ − E₁ = 3.27 × 10¹⁰ J.\n6. (c) Escape needs total energy zero: W = 0 − E₂ = 2.98 × 10¹⁰ J more.\n7. Note: the rotation of the earth is ignored; a launch eastward at the equator contributes up to about 465 m/s of initial speed.","answers":["v = 7.73 × 10³ m/s; T = 5.43 × 10³ s (≈ 90.5 min); a_rad = 8.93 m/s²","W = 3.27 × 10¹⁰ J","additional work to escape = 2.98 × 10¹⁰ J"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 13.6"},{"id":"13.8","title":"Semi-major axis of an asteroid orbit","q":"The asteroid Pallas has an orbital period of 4.62 years and an orbital eccentricity of 0.233. Find the semi-major axis of its orbit. (Sun\u0027s mass m_S = 1.99 × 10³⁰ kg; 1 yr = 3.156 × 10⁷ s.)","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Kepler\u0027s third law for an orbit about the sun: T = 2πa^{3/2}/√(G m_S). The eccentricity is not needed.\n2. T = (4.62 yr)(3.156 × 10⁷ s/yr) = 1.46 × 10⁸ s.\n3. Solve for a: a^{3/2} = √(G m_S) T/(2π), so a = (G m_S T²/4π²)^{1/3}.\n4. G m_S = (6.67 × 10⁻¹¹)(1.99 × 10³⁰) = 1.33 × 10²⁰ m³/s². a³ = (1.33 × 10²⁰)(1.46 × 10⁸)²/(4π²) = 7.2 × 10³⁴ m³.\n5. a = 4.15 × 10¹¹ m (about 2.77 AU).\n6. Evaluate: this lies between the semi-major axes of Mars and Jupiter, as expected for an asteroid in the asteroid belt.","answers":["a = 4.15 × 10¹¹ m (≈ 2.8 AU)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 13.8"},{"id":"13.9","title":"Comet Halley: axis, eccentricity and period","q":"Comet Halley moves in an elongated elliptical orbit around the sun. Its distances from the sun at perihelion and aphelion are 8.75 × 10⁷ km and 5.26 × 10⁹ km, respectively. Find the orbital semi-major axis, eccentricity, and period. (Sun\u0027s mass 1.99 × 10³⁰ kg.)","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. For an ellipse the major axis equals the sum of the perihelion and aphelion distances: 2a = r_p + r_a = 8.75 × 10⁷ km + 5.26 × 10⁹ km, so a = 2.67 × 10⁹ km = 2.67 × 10¹² m.\n2. Perihelion distance r_p = a − ea = a(1 − e): e = 1 − r_p/a = 1 − (8.75 × 10⁷)/(2.67 × 10⁹) = 0.967.\n3. Kepler\u0027s third law: T = 2πa^{3/2}/√(G m_S) = 2π(2.67 × 10¹²)^{3/2}/√(1.33 × 10²⁰) = 2.38 × 10⁹ s = 75.3 yr.\n4. Evaluate: e is close to 1, a very elongated orbit. The comet was at perihelion in early 1986 and returns about 2061.","answers":["a = 2.67 × 10⁹ km","e = 0.967","T = 2.38 × 10⁹ s = 75.3 yr"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 13.9"},{"id":"13.10","title":"Mail pouch falling through a tunnel in the earth","q":"Imagine that we drill a hole through the earth along a diameter and drop a mail pouch of mass m down the hole. Derive an expression for the gravitational force F_g on the pouch as a function of its distance r from the earth\u0027s center. Assume that the earth\u0027s density is uniform (not a very realistic model).","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. At distance r from the center only the mass M within the sphere of radius r attracts the pouch; the shell outside exerts no net force. The attraction is the same as if M were at the center.\n2. For uniform density the mass is proportional to volume: M/m_E = (4/3 π r³)/(4/3 π R_E³) = r³/R_E³, so M = m_E r³/R_E³.\n3. F_g = G M m/r² = G m (m_E r³/R_E³)/r² = G m_E m r/R_E³.\n4. Evaluation: inside a uniform sphere the force is directly proportional to r (not 1/r²); at the surface r = R_E it equals G m_E m/R_E², as it should; at the center it is zero. Since F is a linear restoring force, the pouch would oscillate (simple harmonic motion).","answers":["F_g = G m_E m r/R_E³ (directed toward the center, proportional to r)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 13.10"},{"id":"13.11","title":"Event horizon of a collapsed star","q":"Astrophysical theory suggests that a burned-out star whose mass is at least three solar masses will collapse under its own gravity to form a black hole. If it does, what is the radius of its event horizon? (Sun\u0027s mass 1.99 × 10³⁰ kg; c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m/s.)","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. The event horizon is at the Schwarzschild radius R_S = 2GM/c² (the radius at which the Newtonian escape speed √(2GM/R) equals c).\n2. M = 3(1.99 × 10³⁰ kg) = 6.0 × 10³⁰ kg.\n3. R_S = 2(6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N·m²/kg²)(6.0 × 10³⁰ kg)/(3.00 × 10⁸ m/s)² = 8.9 × 10³ m = 8.9 km (about 5.5 mi).\n4. Evaluate: a mass three times the sun\u0027s would have to be compressed to a sphere only about 9 km in radius.","answers":["R_S = 8.9 × 10³ m = 8.9 km"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 13.11"},{"id":"14.3","title":"Glider launched with initial displacement and velocity","q":"A 0.50 kg glider on a frictionless horizontal air track is attached to a spring of force constant k = 200 N/m (the spring balance reading of Example 14.2: 6.0 N stretches it 0.030 m). The glider is given an initial displacement x₀ = +0.015 m and an initial velocity v₀ₓ = +0.40 m/s. (a) Find the period, amplitude and phase angle of the resulting motion. (b) Write equations for the displacement, velocity and acceleration as functions of time.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: the restoring force is linear, so the motion is SHM. System quantities come from m and k; amplitude and phase come from x₀ and v₀ₓ.\n2. $\\omega=\\sqrt{k/m}=\\sqrt{200/0.50}=20$ rad/s, $T=2\\pi/\\omega=0.314$ s.\n3. Amplitude: $A=\\sqrt{x_0^2+v_{0x}^2/\\omega^2}=\\sqrt{(0.015)^2+(0.40/20)^2}=0.025$ m.\n4. Phase: $\\phi=\\arctan\\!\\left(-\\dfrac{v_{0x}}{\\omega x_0}\\right)=\\arctan(-1.33)=-53^\\circ=-0.93$ rad (the quadrant is fixed by x₀ \u003e 0 giving cos φ \u003e 0 and v₀ₓ \u003e 0 giving sin φ \u003c 0).\n5. (b) $x=(0.025\\ \\text{m})\\cos[(20\\ \\text{rad/s})t-0.93]$; $v_x=-(0.50\\ \\text{m/s})\\sin[(20\\ \\text{rad/s})t-0.93]$; $a_x=-(10\\ \\text{m/s}^2)\\cos[(20\\ \\text{rad/s})t-0.93]$.\n6. Check at t = 0: x = 0.025 cos(−0.93) = 0.015 m and v = +0.40 m/s, as given.","answers":["T = 0.314 s (ω = 20 rad/s)","A = 0.025 m; φ = −0.93 rad (−53°)","x = (0.025 m) cos(20t − 0.93); v = −(0.50 m/s) sin(20t − 0.93); a = −(10 m/s²) cos(20t − 0.93)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 14.3"},{"id":"14.4","title":"Extremes of velocity and acceleration, and energy split","q":"A 0.50 kg glider is attached to a spring of force constant k = 200 N/m on a frictionless horizontal air track. It is pulled 0.020 m from equilibrium and released from rest. (a) Find the maximum and minimum velocities it attains. (b) Find the maximum and minimum accelerations. (c) Find the velocity and acceleration when the glider is halfway from its initial position to the equilibrium position, moving toward equilibrium. (d) Find the total energy, potential energy and kinetic energy at that position.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: the questions refer to positions, so use energy relations $v_x=\\pm\\sqrt{k/m}\\sqrt{A^2-x^2}$ and $a_x=-(k/m)x$ with A = 0.020 m, k/m = 400 s⁻².\n2. (a) $v_{max}=\\sqrt{k/m}\\,A=\\sqrt{400}(0.020)=0.40$ m/s at x = 0; extremes are +0.40 m/s and −0.40 m/s (moving right or left through the origin).\n3. (b) $a_{max}=(k/m)A=400(0.020)=8.0$ m/s² at x = −A; $a_{min}=-8.0$ m/s² at x = +A.\n4. (c) x = A/2 = 0.010 m: $v_x=-\\sqrt{400}\\sqrt{(0.020)^2-(0.010)^2}=-0.35$ m/s (negative, since moving toward the origin); $a_x=-400(0.010)=-4.0$ m/s².\n5. (d) $E=\\tfrac12kA^2=\\tfrac12(200)(0.020)^2=0.040$ J; $U=\\tfrac12k x^2=0.010$ J; $K=\\tfrac12mv^2=\\tfrac12(0.50)(0.35)^2=0.030$ J.\n6. Check: K + U = E; at x = A/2, U is one-fourth of E.","answers":["(a) v_max = +0.40 m/s, v_min = −0.40 m/s","(b) a_max = +8.0 m/s², a_min = −8.0 m/s²","(c) v = −0.35 m/s, a = −4.0 m/s²","(d) E = 0.040 J, U = 0.010 J, K = 0.030 J"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 14.4"},{"id":"14.5","title":"Putty dropped onto an oscillating block","q":"A block of mass M attached to a horizontal spring of force constant k moves in SHM with amplitude A₁ on a frictionless surface. As the block passes through its equilibrium position, a lump of putty of mass m is dropped from a small height and sticks to it. (a) Find the new amplitude and period. (b) Repeat part (a) if the putty is dropped onto the block when it is at one end of its path.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: use energy for the amplitude after the event; the period is a property of the system with its new mass.\n2. (a) Before: $E_1=\\tfrac12kA_1^2=\\tfrac12Mv_1^2$ at x = 0, so $v_1=\\sqrt{k/M}\\,A_1$.\n3. The collision is completely inelastic; horizontal momentum is conserved: $Mv_1=(M+m)v_2$, so $v_2=\\dfrac{M}{M+m}v_1$.\n4. Energy just after (still at x = 0, all kinetic): $E_2=\\tfrac12(M+m)v_2^2=\\dfrac{M}{M+m}E_1$.\n5. With $E_2=\\tfrac12kA_2^2$: $A_2=A_1\\sqrt{\\dfrac{M}{M+m}}$.\n6. Period (system property): $T_2=2\\pi\\sqrt{\\dfrac{M+m}{k}}$.\n7. (b) The block is at rest at x = ±A₁, so adding the putty removes no kinetic energy: the energy stays $\\tfrac12kA_1^2$, hence $A_2=A_1$; the period is the same $T_2=2\\pi\\sqrt{(M+m)/k}$.\n8. Contrast: the same period change in both cases, but only the inelastic collision at the equilibrium position loses energy.","answers":["(a) A₂ = A₁√(M/(M+m)); T = 2π√((M+m)/k)","(b) A₂ = A₁ (unchanged); T = 2π√((M+m)/k)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 14.5"},{"id":"14.6","title":"Bounce of a car with worn-out shock absorbers","q":"The shock absorbers in an old car of mass 1000 kg are completely worn out. When a 980 N person climbs slowly into the car at its center of gravity, the car sinks 2.8 cm. The car (with the person aboard) hits a bump and starts oscillating up and down in SHM. Model the car and person as a single body on a single spring, and find the period and frequency of the oscillation.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: the static sag gives the effective spring constant of the whole suspension; then use $T=2\\pi\\sqrt{m/k}$ with the total mass.\n2. Force constant: $k=-\\dfrac{F_x}{x}=-\\dfrac{980\\ \\text{N}}{-0.028\\ \\text{m}}=3.5\\times10^4$ kg/s² (N/m).\n3. Person\u0027s mass: $w/g=980/9.8=100$ kg; total oscillating mass $m=1000+100=1100$ kg.\n4. Period: $T=2\\pi\\sqrt{\\dfrac{1100}{3.5\\times10^4}}=1.11$ s.\n5. Frequency: $f=1/T=0.90$ Hz.\n6. Check: a period of about 1 s is an unpleasant ride, which is what working shock absorbers (damping) are there to suppress.","answers":["k = 3.5 × 10⁴ N/m","T = 1.11 s","f = 0.90 Hz"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 14.6"},{"id":"14.9","title":"Period of a uniform rod swinging from one end","q":"A uniform rod of mass M and length L is pivoted at one end and swings in a vertical plane through small angles as a physical pendulum. What is its period of oscillation? Compare with the period of a simple pendulum of the same length, and evaluate for a meter stick (L = 1.00 m, g = 9.80 m/s²).","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: physical pendulum, $T=2\\pi\\sqrt{I/(Mgd)}$ for small amplitude, where I is about the pivot and d the pivot-to-center-of-gravity distance.\n2. Uniform rod about an end: $I=\\tfrac13ML^2$; the center of gravity is at $d=L/2$.\n3. $T=2\\pi\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\tfrac13ML^2}{MgL/2}}=2\\pi\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2L}{3g}}$.\n4. Numerically: $T=2\\pi\\sqrt{2(1.00)/(3\\times9.80)}=1.64$ s.\n5. Compare with a simple pendulum of length L, $T_s=2\\pi\\sqrt{L/g}=2.007$ s: ratio $T/T_s=\\sqrt{2/3}=0.816$.\n6. Why: the rod\u0027s I is one-third of mL² and its cg is half as far from the pivot; the two effects together give the factor √(2/3). Equivalent simple-pendulum length is 2L/3.","answers":["T = 2π√(2L/3g)","Meter stick: T = 1.64 s","T/T_simple = √(2/3) = 0.816"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 14.9"},{"id":"14.10","title":"Walking speed of Tyrannosaurus rex from its leg","q":"Suppose a walking animal\u0027s natural pace corresponds to the oscillation of its leg as a physical pendulum. (a) How does the pace depend on the leg length L from hip to foot? Treat the leg as a uniform rod pivoted at the hip joint. (b) Fossil evidence shows that Tyrannosaurus rex had a leg length L = 3.1 m and a stride length S = 4.0 m (the distance from one footprint to the next print of the same foot). Estimate its walking speed. Take g = 9.8 m/s².","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure); a sketch shows a dinosaur with its leg length L from hip to foot and the stride length S between successive prints of the same foot.","sol":"1. Approach: the leg swings as a uniform rod about the hip: $T=2\\pi\\sqrt{2L/3g}$ (Example 14.9).\n2. (a) Each step takes half a period, so the pace in steps per second is $2f=2/T\\propto1/\\sqrt L$: longer legs give a slower pace.\n3. (b) $T=2\\pi\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2(3.1)}{3(9.8)}}=2.9$ s.\n4. The animal covers one stride length S in one period T: $v=S/T=\\dfrac{4.0\\ \\text{m}}{2.9\\ \\text{s}}=1.4$ m/s = 5.0 km/h.\n5. This is about the walking speed of an adult human.\n6. Refinement: legs taper, so the cg is closer than L/2 to the hip and I is well below ML²/3 (about ML²/15 if d is about L/4); the period would be shorter and the speed even greater.","answers":["(a) Pace ∝ 1/√L (longer legs, slower pace)","(b) T ≈ 2.9 s, v ≈ 1.4 m/s (5.0 km/h)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 14.10"},{"id":"15.2","title":"Sinusoidal wave on a clothesline","q":"Cousin Throckmorton holds one end of a taut clothesline and wiggles it up and down sinusoidally with frequency 2.00 Hz and amplitude 0.075 m. The wave speed on the clothesline is 12.0 m/s. At t = 0 Throcky\u0027s end has maximum positive displacement and is instantaneously at rest. Assume that no wave bounces back from the far end. (a) Find the wave amplitude A, angular frequency ω, period T, wavelength λ and wave number k. (b) Write a wave function describing the wave. (c) Write equations for the displacement, as a function of time, of Throcky\u0027s end of the clothesline and of a point 3.00 m from that end.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: kinematics of a sinusoidal wave travelling in +x, with $v=\\lambda f$, $\\omega=2\\pi f$, $k=2\\pi/\\lambda$.\n2. (a) $A=0.075$ m (amplitude of the source); $\\omega=2\\pi f=4.00\\pi$ rad/s = 12.6 rad/s; $T=1/f=0.500$ s; $\\lambda=v/f=12.0/2.00=6.00$ m; $k=2\\pi/\\lambda=1.05$ rad/m (equivalently $k=\\omega/v$).\n3. (b) With y = A at x = 0, t = 0: $y(x,t)=A\\cos(kx-\\omega t)=(0.075\\ \\text{m})\\cos[(1.05\\ \\text{rad/m})x-(12.6\\ \\text{rad/s})t]$.\n4. (c) At x = 0: $y=(0.075\\ \\text{m})\\cos(12.6t)$. At x = 3.00 m: $kx=3.15$ rad = π, so $y=(0.075)\\cos(\\pi-12.6t)=-(0.075\\ \\text{m})\\cos(12.6t)$.\n5. Check: the two points are half a wavelength apart, so they oscillate half a cycle (π rad) out of phase; at t = 0 the v_y = ωA sin(−ωt)|₀ = 0 at x = 0, as stated.","answers":["(a) A = 0.075 m; ω = 12.6 rad/s; T = 0.500 s; λ = 6.00 m; k = 1.05 rad/m","(b) y(x,t) = (0.075 m) cos[(1.05 rad/m)x − (12.6 rad/s)t]","(c) y(0,t) = (0.075 m) cos(12.6t); y(3.00 m,t) = −(0.075 m) cos(12.6t)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 15.2"},{"id":"15.3","title":"Signalling along a rope in a mine shaft","q":"One end of a 2.00 kg rope is tied to a support at the top of a mine shaft 80.0 m deep. The rope is stretched taut by a 20.0 kg box of rocks attached at the bottom. (a) The geologist at the bottom of the shaft signals to a colleague at the top by jerking the rope sideways. What is the speed of a transverse wave on the rope? (b) If a point on the rope is in transverse SHM with f = 2.00 Hz, how many cycles of the wave are there in the rope\u0027s length? Assume the rope is massless for the tension (g = 9.80 m/s²).","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: dynamic relation $v=\\sqrt{F/\\mu}$ for the speed; kinematic relation $v=\\lambda f$ for the wavelength.\n2. Tension from the box: $F=m_{box}g=(20.0)(9.80)=196$ N.\n3. Linear density: $\\mu=m_{rope}/L=2.00/80.0=0.0250$ kg/m.\n4. (a) $v=\\sqrt{196/0.0250}=88.5$ m/s.\n5. (b) $\\lambda=v/f=88.5/2.00=44.3$ m; the number of wavelengths in the rope is $L/\\lambda=80.0/44.3=1.81$.\n6. Evaluation: the rope\u0027s own weight (10% of the box) makes the real tension larger at the top, so v and λ increase going up (v at the top is about 92.9 m/s).","answers":["(a) v = 88.5 m/s","(b) λ = 44.3 m; 1.81 wavelengths (cycles) in the rope"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 15.3"},{"id":"15.4","title":"Power fed into a clothesline","q":"Cousin Throckmorton sends a sinusoidal wave down a clothesline: frequency 2.00 Hz, amplitude 0.075 m, wave speed 12.0 m/s. The linear mass density of the clothesline is 0.250 kg/m and Throcky applies tension 36.0 N. (a) At what maximum rate does he put energy into the clothesline, that is, what is his maximum instantaneous power? (b) What is his average power? (c) As Throcky tires, the amplitude decreases. What is the average power when the amplitude is 7.50 mm?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: instantaneous power of a sinusoidal wave on a string $P=\\sqrt{\\mu F}\\,\\omega^2A^2\\sin^2(kx-\\omega t)$; the maximum is when sin² = 1 and the average is half of that.\n2. $\\omega=2\\pi f=4.00\\pi$ rad/s; $\\sqrt{\\mu F}=\\sqrt{(0.250)(36.0)}=3.00$ kg/s.\n3. (a) $P_{max}=\\sqrt{\\mu F}\\,\\omega^2A^2=(3.00)(12.57)^2(0.075)^2=2.66$ W.\n4. (b) $P_{av}=\\tfrac12P_{max}=1.33$ W.\n5. (c) The amplitude is 1/10 of the original, and P_av ∝ A², so $P_{av}=(1/10)^2(1.33)=0.0133$ W = 13.3 mW.\n6. Check: P_max occurs twice per period at each point (sin² = 1); the minimum instantaneous power is zero.","answers":["(a) P_max = 2.66 W","(b) P_av = 1.33 W","(c) P_av = 13.3 mW"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 15.4"},{"id":"15.5","title":"Siren intensity versus distance","q":"A siren on a tall pole radiates sound waves uniformly in all directions. At a distance of 15.0 m from the siren the sound intensity is 0.250 W/m². At what distance is the intensity only 0.010 W/m²? Ignore reflections from the ground.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: a point source radiating uniformly has total power P spread over a sphere, $I=P/4\\pi r^2$, so $I_1/I_2=r_2^2/r_1^2$ (inverse-square law).\n2. Solve for the distance: $r_2=r_1\\sqrt{I_1/I_2}$.\n3. $r_2=(15.0\\ \\text{m})\\sqrt{0.250/0.010}=(15.0)\\sqrt{25}=75.0$ m.\n4. Check: I₂ is 25 times smaller than I₁, and the distance is five times larger, consistent with 1/5² = 1/25.","answers":["r₂ = 75.0 m"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 15.5"},{"id":"15.6","title":"Standing wave on a guitar string","q":"A guitar string lies along the x-axis when in equilibrium. The end of the string at x = 0 (the bridge) is fixed. A sinusoidal wave with amplitude A = 7.50 × 10⁻⁴ m (0.750 mm) and frequency f = 440 Hz travels along the string in the −x direction at v = 143 m/s. It is reflected from the fixed end, and the superposition of the incident and reflected waves forms a standing wave. (a) Find the equation giving the displacement of a point on the string as a function of position and time. (b) Locate the nodes. (c) Find the amplitude of the standing wave and the maximum transverse velocity and acceleration.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: reflection at a fixed end inverts the wave, so the standing wave is $y=(A_{SW}\\sin kx)\\sin\\omega t$ with $A_{SW}=2A$.\n2. $A_{SW}=2(0.750)=1.50$ mm; $\\omega=2\\pi f=2760$ rad/s; $k=\\omega/v=2760/143=19.3$ rad/m.\n3. (a) $y(x,t)=[(1.50\\times10^{-3}\\ \\text{m})\\sin(19.3\\ \\text{rad/m}\\ x)]\\sin(2760\\ \\text{rad/s}\\ t)$.\n4. (b) Nodes at $x=n\\lambda/2$ with $\\lambda=v/f=143/440=0.325$ m: $x=0,\\ 0.163,\\ 0.325,\\ 0.488,\\dots$ m.\n5. (c) The maximum displacement is the amplitude of the antinodes, 1.50 mm, at $x=0.081,\\ 0.244,\\ 0.406,\\dots$ m (midway between nodes).\n6. Transverse velocity: $v_y=\\partial y/\\partial t=[(4.15\\ \\text{m/s})\\sin kx]\\cos\\omega t$, so the maximum at an antinode is 4.15 m/s. Acceleration: $a_y=-[(1.15\\times10^4\\ \\text{m/s}^2)\\sin kx]\\sin\\omega t$, so the maximum is $1.15\\times10^4$ m/s² (about 1170 g).","answers":["(a) y = [(1.50 mm) sin(19.3 x)] sin(2760 t) (x in m, t in s)","(b) nodes at x = 0, 0.163, 0.325, 0.488 m, ... (every λ/2 = 0.163 m)","(c) amplitude 1.50 mm; v_y,max = 4.15 m/s; a_y,max = 1.15 × 10⁴ m/s²"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 15.6"},{"id":"15.7","title":"Harmonics of a giant bass viol string","q":"In an attempt to get your name in Guinness World Records, you build a bass viol with strings of length 5.00 m between fixed points. One string, with linear mass density 40.0 g/m, is tuned to a 20.0 Hz fundamental frequency (the lowest frequency that the human ear can hear). Calculate (a) the tension of this string, (b) the frequency and wavelength on the string of the second harmonic, and (c) the frequency and wavelength on the string of the second overtone.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: for a string fixed at both ends $f_n=nv/2L=nf_1$ and $\\lambda_n=2L/n$, with $v=\\sqrt{F/\\mu}$ so $f_1=\\tfrac1{2L}\\sqrt{F/\\mu}$.\n2. (a) Solve for the tension: $F=4\\mu L^2f_1^2=4(0.0400)(5.00)^2(20.0)^2=1600$ N (360 lb).\n3. (b) The second harmonic, n = 2: $f_2=2f_1=40.0$ Hz, $\\lambda_2=2L/2=5.00$ m.\n4. (c) The second overtone is the third harmonic, n = 3: $f_3=3f_1=60.0$ Hz, $\\lambda_3=2L/3=3.33$ m.\n5. Check: λ₂ equals the string length and λ₃ is two-thirds of it, matching the mode patterns; wave speed on the string is $2Lf_1=200$ m/s.","answers":["(a) F = 1600 N","(b) f₂ = 40.0 Hz, λ₂ = 5.00 m","(c) f₃ = 60.0 Hz, λ₃ = 3.33 m"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 15.7"},{"id":"15.8","title":"Sound from the vibrating bass-viol string","q":"What are the frequency and wavelength of the sound waves produced in the air when the 5.00 m bass-viol string of Example 15.7 (fundamental frequency 20.0 Hz, string tension giving a wave speed on the string of 200 m/s) is vibrating at its fundamental frequency? The speed of sound in air at 20 °C is 344 m/s. Compare the wavelengths on the string and in the air.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: the string forces the surrounding air to vibrate at its own frequency, so the sound frequency equals the string\u0027s frequency. The wavelength in air follows from the speed of sound.\n2. $f_{sound}=f_1=20.0$ Hz.\n3. $\\lambda_{sound}=v_{sound}/f_1=344/20.0=17.2$ m.\n4. The fundamental wavelength on the string is $\\lambda_1=2L=10.0$ m, so the wavelength in air is larger by the factor 17.2/10.0 = 1.72.\n5. Check: with the same frequency the wavelengths scale with the speeds, 344/200 = 1.72.","answers":["f = 20.0 Hz","λ_air = 17.2 m (1.72 times the 10.0 m wavelength on the string)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 15.8"},{"id":"16.1","title":"Displacement amplitude of a moderate sound wave","q":"In a sinusoidal sound wave of moderate loudness, the maximum pressure variations are about 3.0 × 10⁻² Pa above and below atmospheric pressure. Find the corresponding maximum displacement if the frequency is 1000 Hz. In air at normal atmospheric pressure and density, the speed of sound is 344 m/s and the bulk modulus is 1.42 × 10⁵ Pa.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: relate the pressure amplitude to the displacement amplitude with $p_{max}=BkA$ (the ear senses pressure, while the wave is also a displacement wave).\n2. Wave number: $k=\\omega/v=2\\pi f/v=(2\\pi\\ \\text{rad})(1000\\ \\text{Hz})/(344\\ \\text{m/s})=18.3$ rad/m.\n3. Displacement amplitude: $A=\\dfrac{p_{max}}{Bk}=\\dfrac{3.0\\times10^{-2}\\ \\text{Pa}}{(1.42\\times10^5\\ \\text{Pa})(18.3\\ \\text{rad/m})}=1.2\\times10^{-8}$ m.\n4. Check: this is only about 1/100 the size of a human cell, so the ear detects such displacements only indirectly, through pressure.","answers":["A = 1.2 × 10⁻⁸ m (k = 18.3 rad/m)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 16.1"},{"id":"16.4","title":"Speed of sound in air and audible wavelengths","q":"Find the speed of sound in air at T = 20 °C, and find the range of wavelengths in air to which the human ear (which can hear frequencies in the range of 20 Hz to 20 000 Hz) is sensitive. The mean molar mass for air (a mixture of mostly nitrogen and oxygen) is M = 28.8 × 10⁻³ kg/mol, and the ratio of heat capacities is γ = 1.40. Use R = 8.314 J/(mol·K).","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: speed of sound in an ideal gas, $v=\\sqrt{\\gamma RT/M}$ with T in kelvin; then $\\lambda=v/f$ for the frequency limits.\n2. $T=20^\\circ\\text{C}=293$ K.\n3. $v=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{(1.40)(8.314)(293)}{28.8\\times10^{-3}}}=344$ m/s.\n4. Wavelengths: for 20 Hz, $\\lambda=344/20=17$ m; for 20 000 Hz, $\\lambda=344/20\\,000=1.7$ cm.\n5. Check: the computed v agrees with the measured value at 20 °C to within 0.3%.","answers":["v = 344 m/s","Audible wavelengths from 17 m (20 Hz) to 1.7 cm (20 kHz)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 16.4"},{"id":"16.9","title":"Level drop when moving away from a bird","q":"Consider an idealized bird (treated as a point source) that emits constant sound power, with intensity obeying the inverse-square law. If you move twice the distance from the bird, by how many decibels does the sound intensity level drop?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure); two listeners P₁ and P₂ stand at distances r₁ and r₂ = 2r₁ from the bird.","sol":"1. Approach: the level difference depends only on the ratio of intensities, since $\\beta=(10\\ \\text{dB})\\log(I/I_0)$.\n2. $\\beta_2-\\beta_1=(10\\ \\text{dB})\\left(\\log\\dfrac{I_2}{I_0}-\\log\\dfrac{I_1}{I_0}\\right)=(10\\ \\text{dB})\\log\\dfrac{I_2}{I_1}$.\n3. Inverse-square law: $I_2/I_1=r_1^2/r_2^2=\\tfrac14$.\n4. $\\beta_2-\\beta_1=(10\\ \\text{dB})\\log\\tfrac14=-6.0$ dB.\n5. Check: the answer does not depend on the initial level; any doubling of distance from such a source lowers the level by 6 dB.","answers":["Δβ = −6.0 dB (the level drops by 6.0 dB)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 16.9"},{"id":"16.11","title":"Stopped pipe and open pipe with equal wavelengths","q":"On a day when the speed of sound is 345 m/s, the fundamental frequency of a particular stopped organ pipe is 220 Hz. (a) How long is this pipe? (b) The second overtone of this pipe has the same wavelength as the third harmonic of an open pipe. How long is the open pipe?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: stopped pipe $f_n=nv/4L$ with n = 1, 3, 5, ...; open pipe $f_n=nv/2L$ with n = 1, 2, 3, ...\n2. (a) $f_1=v/4L$, so $L_{stopped}=\\dfrac{v}{4f_1}=\\dfrac{345}{4(220)}=0.392$ m.\n3. (b) The second overtone of the stopped pipe is its third allowed frequency, n = 5: $f_5=5f_1=1100$ Hz.\n4. Same wavelength in the same air means the same frequency; the third harmonic of the open pipe is $3f_1^{open}=3v/2L_{open}=1100$ Hz.\n5. $L_{open}=\\dfrac{3(345)}{2(1100)}=0.470$ m.\n6. Check: the longer open pipe has fundamental $1100/3=367$ Hz, higher than the 220 Hz of the shorter stopped pipe, because an open pipe\u0027s fundamental has λ = 2L, while a stopped pipe\u0027s has λ = 4L.","answers":["(a) L = 0.392 m","(b) L_open = 0.470 m (fundamental 367 Hz)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 16.11"},{"id":"16.13","title":"Constructive and destructive interference from two speakers","q":"Two small loudspeakers, A and B, are driven by the same amplifier and emit pure sinusoidal waves in phase. (a) For what frequencies does constructive interference occur at point P? (b) For what frequencies does destructive interference occur? The speed of sound is 350 m/s.","figs":["figures/ex-phys/ex16-23.webp"],"figDesc":"Perspective sketch: speakers A and B stand 2.00 m and 1.00 m on opposite sides of a point on the line joining them; the microphone P is 4.00 m from that point along a line perpendicular to the speaker line.","sol":"1. Approach: the interference at P depends on the path difference d. Constructive if d = nλ, destructive if d = (n + ½)λ; use v = fλ.\n2. Path lengths (Pythagoras): $AP=\\sqrt{(2.00)^2+(4.00)^2}=4.47$ m; $BP=\\sqrt{(1.00)^2+(4.00)^2}=4.12$ m. $d=4.47-4.12=0.35$ m.\n3. (a) Constructive: $d=n\\lambda=nv/f$, so $f_n=\\dfrac{nv}{d}=n\\,\\dfrac{350}{0.35}=1000,\\ 2000,\\ 3000,\\dots$ Hz ($n=1,2,3,\\dots$).\n4. (b) Destructive: $d=(n-\\tfrac12)\\lambda$, i.e. $d=\\lambda/2,\\ 3\\lambda/2,\\dots$, so $f_n=\\dfrac{nv}{2d}=n\\,\\dfrac{350}{2(0.35)}=500,\\ 1500,\\ 2500,\\dots$ Hz ($n=1,3,5,\\dots$).\n5. Check: as the frequency is increased, the level at P alternates between maxima and minima at these frequencies; room reflections blur the effect indoors, and an anechoic chamber shows it best.","answers":["(a) f = 1000, 2000, 3000, ... Hz (f = n·350/0.35)","(b) f = 500, 1500, 2500, ... Hz (odd multiples of 500 Hz)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 16.13"},{"id":"16.17","title":"Doppler shift for moving siren and moving listener","q":"A siren emits a sinusoidal sound of frequency 300 Hz. The speed of sound is 340 m/s and the air is still. The siren is moving away from the listener with a speed of 45 m/s relative to the air, and the listener is moving toward the siren with a speed of 15 m/s relative to the air. What frequency does the listener hear?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure); a siren and a listener on a line; the siren moves away from the listener, the listener moves toward the siren, so both velocities point from listener to source.","sol":"1. Approach: Doppler formula $f_L=\\dfrac{v+v_L}{v+v_S}f_S$, with all velocities taken positive in the direction from listener to source.\n2. The source moves away from the listener, i.e. along the direction from listener to source: $v_S=+45$ m/s. The listener moves toward the source, also along that direction: $v_L=+15$ m/s.\n3. $f_L=\\dfrac{340+15}{340+45}(300\\ \\text{Hz})=\\dfrac{355}{385}(300)=277$ Hz.\n4. Check: source and listener are separating at 30 m/s, so $f_L\u003cf_S$. The heard frequency depends on the motions relative to the air, not just on the relative motion (compare 276 Hz for a source moving away alone and 274 Hz for a listener moving away alone, both at 30 m/s).","answers":["f_L ≈ 277 Hz (lower than the emitted 300 Hz)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 16.17"},{"id":"16.18","title":"Echo frequency from a warehouse wall","q":"A police car with its siren (frequency 300 Hz) on is moving toward a warehouse at 30 m/s. The speed of sound is 340 m/s and the air is still. What frequency does the driver hear reflected from the warehouse?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure); the car approaches a stationary warehouse wall that reflects the siren sound back to the car.","sol":"1. Approach: two Doppler shifts. First the stationary warehouse acts as a listener of the moving source; then it acts as a stationary source of frequency f_W for the moving listener (the driver).\n2. Stage 1 (car → warehouse): source moving toward the listener, so with positive direction from listener to source $v_S=-30$ m/s, $v_L=0$: $f_W=\\dfrac{v}{v+v_S}f_S=\\dfrac{340}{340-30}(300)=329$ Hz.\n3. Stage 2 (warehouse → car): source at rest ($v_S=0$), listener moving toward it ($v_L=+30$ m/s): $f_L=\\dfrac{v+v_L}{v}f_W=\\dfrac{340+30}{340}(329)=358$ Hz.\n4. Check: both shifts increase the frequency, so the echo is higher than the 329 Hz a stationary listener at the warehouse would hear.","answers":["Frequency reaching the warehouse: 329 Hz","Frequency heard by the driver: 358 Hz"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 16.18"},{"id":"16.19","title":"Delay of the sonic boom from a supersonic airplane","q":"An airplane is flying at Mach 1.75 at an altitude of 8000 m, where the speed of sound is 320 m/s. How long after the plane passes directly overhead will you hear the sonic boom?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure); a conical shock wave trails behind the airplane; the observer is on the ground below the flight path.","sol":"1. Approach: the shock wave is a cone trailing the plane with half-angle α given by $\\sin\\alpha=v/v_S=1/(\\text{Mach number})$. You hear the boom when the cone reaches you, after the plane has travelled a distance $v_St$ beyond the overhead point.\n2. $\\alpha=\\arcsin(1/1.75)=34.8^\\circ$.\n3. Plane speed: $v_S=(1.75)(320)=560$ m/s.\n4. Geometry: the cone surface through the observer makes angle α with the flight line, so $\\tan\\alpha=\\dfrac{h}{v_St}$ with h = 8000 m.\n5. $t=\\dfrac{h}{v_S\\tan\\alpha}=\\dfrac{8000}{(560)(\\tan34.8^\\circ)}=20.6$ s.\n6. Check: the boom arrives about 20 s after the plane has passed, when it is already some 11 km away.","answers":["α = 34.8°; v_S = 560 m/s","t ≈ 20.6 s after the plane passes overhead"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 16.19"},{"id":"17.3","title":"Mercury overflowing from a heated glass flask","q":"A glass flask of volume 200 cm³ is filled to the brim with mercury at 20°C. How much mercury overflows when the temperature of the system is raised to 100°C? The coefficient of linear expansion of the glass is 0.40×10⁻⁵ K⁻¹; the coefficient of volume expansion of mercury is 18×10⁻⁵ K⁻¹.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: the overflow is the difference between the volume increases of the mercury and of the flask\u0027s interior; both follow $\\Delta V=\\beta V_0\\Delta T$.\n2. Glass: $\\beta_{glass}=3\\alpha_{glass}=3(0.40\\times10^{-5})=1.2\\times10^{-5}\\,\\mathrm{K^{-1}}$ (the interior of a container expands like solid glass).\n3. Temperature change $\\Delta T=80$ K.\n4. $\\Delta V_{Hg}-\\Delta V_{glass}=V_0\\Delta T(\\beta_{Hg}-\\beta_{glass})=(200\\ \\mathrm{cm^3})(80)(18\\times10^{-5}-1.2\\times10^{-5})$.\n5. Result 2.7 cm³. Evaluate: this is how a mercury-in-glass thermometer works, since mercury expands faster than glass.","answers":["Overflow ≈ 2.7 cm³"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 17.3"},{"id":"17.4","title":"Aluminum spacer pressed between rigid walls","q":"An aluminum cylinder 10 cm long, with a cross-sectional area of 20 cm², is used as a spacer between two steel walls. At 17.2°C it just slips between the walls. Calculate the stress in the cylinder and the total force it exerts on each wall when it warms to 22.3°C, assuming that the walls are perfectly rigid and a constant distance apart. (For aluminum: Young\u0027s modulus Y = 7.0×10¹⁰ Pa, α = 2.4×10⁻⁵ K⁻¹.)","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: the cylinder would expand freely by $\\alpha\\Delta T$ per unit length, but the walls hold the length constant, so an elastic compressive strain exactly cancels it.\n2. Condition for constant length: $\\alpha\\Delta T+\\dfrac{F}{AY}=0$ so $\\dfrac{F}{A}=-Y\\alpha\\Delta T$.\n3. $\\Delta T=22.3-17.2=5.1$ K.\n4. Stress: $-(7.0\\times10^{10})(2.4\\times10^{-5})(5.1)=-8.6\\times10^{6}$ Pa (compressive).\n5. Force: $F=A(F/A)=(20\\times10^{-4}\\ \\mathrm{m^2})(-8.6\\times10^6)=-1.7\\times10^4$ N, about 1.9 tons.\n6. Check: the 10 cm length is irrelevant; the force is huge for a 5 K change, so thermal stress must be designed for.","answers":["Stress ≈ −8.6×10⁶ Pa (compressive, ≈ 1200 lb/in²)","Force on each wall ≈ 1.7×10⁴ N"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 17.4"},{"id":"17.8","title":"Ice needed to cool a drink to 0°C","q":"A glass contains 0.25 kg of Omni-Cola (mostly water) initially at 25°C. How much ice, initially at −20°C, must you add to obtain a final temperature of 0°C with all the ice melted? Neglect the heat capacity of the glass. (c_water = 4190 J/kg·K, c_ice = 2100 J/kg·K, L_f = 3.34×10⁵ J/kg.)","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: calorimetry, $\\sum Q=0$, with three terms: cooling the cola, warming the ice to 0°C, and melting the ice at 0°C.\n2. Cola: $Q_C=m_Cc_W(T-T_{0C})=(0.25)(4190)(0-25)=-2.62\\times10^4$ J.\n3. Ice warming: $Q_I=m_Ic_I(0-(-20))=m_I(2100)(20)$.\n4. Melting: $Q_2=m_IL_f$.\n5. Sum zero: $m_I[c_I(T-T_{0I})+L_f]=-m_Cc_W(T-T_{0C})$, so $m_I=\\dfrac{m_Cc_W(T_{0C}-T)}{c_I(T-T_{0I})+L_f}$.\n6. $m_I=\\dfrac{(0.25)(4190)(25)}{(2100)(20)+3.34\\times10^5}=0.070$ kg.\n7. Check: about three or four ice cubes (70 g) for 250 g of drink is reasonable.","answers":["m_ice ≈ 0.070 kg (70 g)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 17.8"},{"id":"17.9","title":"Hot copper pot with cool water and a sealed lid","q":"A hot copper pot of mass 2.0 kg (including its copper lid) is at a temperature of 150°C. You pour 0.10 kg of cool water at 25°C into the pot, then quickly replace the lid so no steam can escape. Find the final temperature of the pot and its contents, and determine the phase of the water (liquid, gas, or a mixture). Assume that no heat is lost to the surroundings. (c_Cu = 390 J/kg·K, c_water = 4190 J/kg·K, L_v = 2.256×10⁶ J/kg.)","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: three possible outcomes: (1) no boiling, T \u003c 100°C; (2) part of the water boils, T = 100°C; (3) all boils, T \u003e 100°C. Test them in order.\n2. Case 1: $T=\\dfrac{m_Wc_WT_{0W}+m_{Cu}c_{Cu}T_{0Cu}}{m_Wc_W+m_{Cu}c_{Cu}}=\\dfrac{(0.10)(4190)(25)+(2.0)(390)(150)}{(0.10)(4190)+(2.0)(390)}=106$°C. This exceeds 100°C, so some water boils.\n3. Case 2: final T = 100°C and a fraction $x$ boils: $m_Wc_W(100-25)+x\\,m_WL_v+m_{Cu}c_{Cu}(100-150)=0$.\n4. $x=\\dfrac{-m_{Cu}c_{Cu}(100-150)-m_Wc_W(100-25)}{m_WL_v}=\\dfrac{39000-31400}{(0.10)(2.256\\times10^6)}=0.034$.\n5. Since 0 \u003c x \u003c 1, case 2 holds.\n6. Check: all the water would vaporize only if less than about 15 g were poured in.","answers":["Final temperature 100°C","3.4% of the water (about 3.4 g) is steam; the rest is liquid water"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 17.9"},{"id":"17.10","title":"Gasoline needed to heat and boil water on a stove","q":"In a particular camp stove, only 30% of the energy released in burning gasoline goes to heating the water in a pot on the stove. How much gasoline must we burn to heat 1.00 L (1.00 kg) of water from 20°C to 100°C and boil away 0.25 kg of it? (Heat of combustion of gasoline 4.6×10⁷ J/kg; c_water = 4190 J/kg·K; L_v = 2.256×10⁶ J/kg.)","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: add the heat to warm all the water and the heat to vaporize part of it; this is only 30% of the heat of combustion.\n2. Warming: $Q_1=mc\\Delta T=(1.00)(4190)(80)=3.35\\times10^5$ J.\n3. Boiling: $Q_2=mL_v=(0.25)(2.256\\times10^6)=5.64\\times10^5$ J.\n4. Total useful $Q=8.99\\times10^5$ J, which is 30% of the combustion heat: $Q_{comb}=8.99\\times10^5/0.30=3.00\\times10^6$ J.\n5. Mass of gasoline: $m=Q_{comb}/L_c=3.00\\times10^6/(4.6\\times10^7\\ \\mathrm{J/kg})=0.065$ kg = 65 g, about 0.09 L.\n6. Note that boiling consumes more energy than heating.","answers":["Heat needed ≈ 8.99×10⁵ J; heat of combustion needed ≈ 3.00×10⁶ J","Gasoline ≈ 65 g (about 0.09 L)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 17.10"},{"id":"17.12","title":"Steady conduction through steel and copper bars in series","q":"A steel bar 10.0 cm long is welded end to end to a copper bar 20.0 cm long. Each bar has a square cross section, 2.00 cm on a side. The free end of the steel bar is kept at 100°C by placing it in contact with steam, and the free end of the copper bar is kept at 0°C by placing it in contact with ice. Both bars are perfectly insulated on their sides. Find the steady-state temperature at the junction of the two bars and the total rate of heat flow through the bars. (k_steel = 50.2 W/m·K, k_copper = 385 W/m·K.)","figs":["figures/ex-phys/ex17-12.webp"],"figDesc":"Steel bar (10.0 cm long) joined to a copper bar (20.0 cm long) between two walls; both bars have a 2.00 cm square cross section; the steel end is at T_H = 100°C, the copper end at T_C = 0°C; the junction temperature T is unknown.","sol":"1. Approach: in steady state the same heat current flows through both bars: $H_S=H_{Cu}$.\n2. $k_SA\\dfrac{T_H-T}{L_S}=k_{Cu}A\\dfrac{T-T_C}{L_{Cu}}$; the area cancels.\n3. Solve: $T=\\dfrac{(k_S/L_S)T_H+(k_{Cu}/L_{Cu})T_C}{k_S/L_S+k_{Cu}/L_{Cu}}$.\n4. Numbers: $k_S/L_S=502$, $k_{Cu}/L_{Cu}=1925$ W/m²K, so $T=\\dfrac{502(100)+1925(0)}{502+1925}=20.7$°C.\n5. Heat current: $H_S=(50.2)(0.0200)^2\\dfrac{100-20.7}{0.100}=15.9$ W, and $H_{Cu}=(385)(0.0200)^2\\dfrac{20.7}{0.200}=15.9$ W.\n6. Check: the steel bar, though shorter, takes most of the temperature drop because it is the poorer conductor (its thermal resistance is larger).","answers":["Junction temperature ≈ 20.7°C","Heat current ≈ 15.9 W"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 17.12"},{"id":"17.15","title":"Net radiative heat loss of a human body","q":"What is the total rate of radiation of energy from a human body with surface area 1.20 m² and surface temperature 30°C = 303 K? If the surroundings are at a temperature of 20°C, what is the net rate of radiative heat loss from the body? The emissivity of the human body is very close to unity, irrespective of skin pigmentation. (σ = 5.67×10⁻⁸ W/m²·K⁴.)","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: the body emits $H=Ae\\sigma T^4$ and absorbs radiation from its surroundings at $T_s$; the net loss is the difference.\n2. Emission with e = 1: $H=(1.20)(1)(5.67\\times10^{-8})(303)^4=574$ W.\n3. Net: $H_{net}=Ae\\sigma(T^4-T_s^4)=(1.20)(5.67\\times10^{-8})[(303)^4-(293)^4]$.\n4. $(303)^4-(293)^4\\approx 8.43\\times10^9-7.37\\times10^9=1.06\\times10^9$ K⁴, giving about 72 W.\n5. Check: positive net value means heat flows from the warm body to the cooler surroundings; temperatures must be in kelvins.","answers":["Total emission ≈ 574 W","Net radiative loss ≈ 72 W"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 17.15"},{"id":"18.2","title":"Temperature after compressing an engine charge","q":"In an automobile engine, a mixture of air and vaporized gasoline is compressed in the cylinders before being ignited. A typical engine has a compression ratio of 9.00 to 1; that is, the gas in the cylinders is compressed to 1/9.00 of its original volume. The intake and exhaust valves are closed during the compression, so the quantity of gas is constant. What is the final temperature of the compressed gas if its initial temperature is 27°C and the initial and final pressures are 1.00 atm and 21.7 atm, respectively?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: the same quantity of ideal gas in two states, so $p_1V_1/T_1=p_2V_2/T_2$; the cylinder volumes are not needed, only $V_1=9.00\\,V_2$.\n2. Initial: $T_1=27+273=300$ K, $p_1=1.00$ atm. Final: $p_2=21.7$ atm, $V_2=V_1/9.00$.\n3. Solve: $T_2=T_1\\dfrac{p_2V_2}{p_1V_1}=(300\\ \\mathrm{K})\\dfrac{(21.7)(V_2)}{(1.00)(9.00\\,V_2)}$.\n4. $T_2=723$ K $=450$°C.\n5. Check: pressure units cancel, so atm is fine; this is the temperature before ignition, and burning raises it further.","answers":["T₂ ≈ 723 K ≈ 450°C"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 18.2"},{"id":"18.3","title":"Mass of air added to a scuba tank","q":"An \u0027empty\u0027 aluminum scuba tank contains 11.0 L of air at 21°C and 1 atm. When the tank is filled rapidly from a compressor, the air temperature is 42°C and the gauge pressure is 2.10×10⁷ Pa. What mass of air was added? (Air is about 78% nitrogen, 21% oxygen, and 1% miscellaneous; its average molar mass is 28.8 g/mol = 28.8×10⁻³ kg/mol. 1 atm = 1.013×10⁵ Pa.)","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: find the moles of air in the initial and final states from $pV=nRT$ (volume unchanged), then convert the difference to mass with $m=nM$.\n2. Convert: $T_1=294$ K, $T_2=315$ K; absolute final pressure $p_2=2.10\\times10^7+1.013\\times10^5=2.11\\times10^7$ Pa; $V=11.0\\times10^{-3}$ m³.\n3. Initial: $n_1=\\dfrac{p_1V}{RT_1}=\\dfrac{(1.013\\times10^5)(11.0\\times10^{-3})}{(8.314)(294)}=0.46$ mol.\n4. Final: $n_2=\\dfrac{p_2V}{RT_2}=\\dfrac{(2.11\\times10^7)(11.0\\times10^{-3})}{(8.314)(315)}=88.6$ mol.\n5. Added: $n_2-n_1=88.1$ mol, so $m=(28.8\\times10^{-3})(88.1)=2.54$ kg.\n6. Check: the gauge pressure must be converted to absolute pressure; 2.5 kg is easily weighed.","answers":["Moles added ≈ 88.1 mol","Mass added ≈ 2.54 kg"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 18.3"},{"id":"18.4","title":"Pressure of an isothermal atmosphere versus height","q":"Find the variation of atmospheric pressure with elevation in the earth\u0027s atmosphere. Assume that at all elevations, T = 0°C and g = 9.80 m/s². Take the molar mass of air as M = 28.8×10⁻³ kg/mol and the sea-level pressure as p₀ = 1.013×10⁵ Pa. Evaluate the pressure at the summit of Mount Everest, y = 8863 m.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: two unknowns p(y) and ρ(y); use the fluid-statics relation $dp/dy=-\\rho g$ together with the ideal-gas density $\\rho=pM/(RT)$.\n2. Combine: $\\dfrac{dp}{dy}=-\\dfrac{pM}{RT}g$, so $\\dfrac{dp}{p}=-\\dfrac{Mg}{RT}dy$.\n3. Integrate from $y_1=0$ ($p_1=p_0$) to $y$: $\\ln\\dfrac{p}{p_0}=-\\dfrac{Mgy}{RT}$, so $p=p_0e^{-Mgy/(RT)}$.\n4. Everest: $\\dfrac{Mgy}{RT}=\\dfrac{(28.8\\times10^{-3})(9.80)(8863)}{(8.314)(273)}=1.10$.\n5. $p=(1.013\\times10^5\\ \\mathrm{Pa})e^{-1.10}=0.337\\times10^5$ Pa $=0.33$ atm.\n6. Check: pressure falls exponentially, slope $dp/dy=-\\rho g$ shrinks with height; real T and g vary, but the result explains the need for oxygen and cabin pressurization.","answers":["p = p₀ e^(−Mgy/RT)","At y = 8863 m: p ≈ 0.337×10⁵ Pa ≈ 0.33 atm"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 18.4"},{"id":"18.6","title":"Molecular kinetic energy and rms speed at 27°C","q":"(a) What is the average translational kinetic energy of an ideal-gas molecule at 27°C? (b) What is the total random translational kinetic energy of the molecules in 1 mole of this gas? (c) What is the root-mean-square speed of oxygen molecules at this temperature? (Molar mass of O₂ is 32.0 g/mol; Boltzmann constant k = 1.381×10⁻²³ J/K; N_A = 6.022×10²³ molecules/mol.)","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: use $\\tfrac12m(v^2)_{av}=\\tfrac32kT$ per molecule, $K_{tr}=\\tfrac32nRT$ per mole, and $v_{rms}=\\sqrt{3RT/M}$; $T=300$ K.\n2. (a) $\\tfrac32kT=\\tfrac32(1.381\\times10^{-23})(300)=6.21\\times10^{-21}$ J.\n3. (b) $K_{tr}=\\tfrac32nRT=\\tfrac32(1)(8.314)(300)=3740$ J.\n4. (c) $M=32.0\\times10^{-3}$ kg/mol: $v_{rms}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3(8.314)(300)}{32.0\\times10^{-3}}}=484$ m/s (1740 km/h).\n5. Check (b): $N_A$ times (a) = $(6.022\\times10^{23})(6.21\\times10^{-21})=3740$ J. The result in (a) does not depend on molecular mass.","answers":["(a) 6.21×10⁻²¹ J","(b) 3740 J","(c) v_rms ≈ 484 m/s"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 18.6"},{"id":"18.7","title":"Rms and average speed of five molecules","q":"Five gas molecules chosen at random are found to have speeds of 500, 600, 700, 800 and 900 m/s. What is the rms speed? What is the average speed?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: rms speed is the square root of the mean of the squares; the average speed is the ordinary mean.\n2. Mean of squares: $(v^2)_{av}=\\dfrac{500^2+600^2+700^2+800^2+900^2}{5}=5.10\\times10^5$ m²/s².\n3. $v_{rms}=\\sqrt{5.10\\times10^5}=714$ m/s.\n4. Average speed: $v_{av}=\\dfrac{500+600+700+800+900}{5}=700$ m/s.\n5. Check: $v_{rms}\u003ev_{av}$ because squaring weights higher speeds more heavily; they are equal only if all speeds are equal.","answers":["v_rms ≈ 714 m/s","v_av = 700 m/s"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 18.7"},{"id":"18.8","title":"Mean free path and collision rate in air","q":"(a) Estimate the mean free path of a molecule of air at 27°C and 1 atm. Model the molecules as spheres with radius r = 2.0×10⁻¹⁰ m. (b) Estimate the mean free time of an oxygen molecule with v = v_rms at 27°C and 1 atm, given that its rms speed there is 484 m/s. (k = 1.38×10⁻²³ J/K, 1 atm = 1.01×10⁵ Pa.)","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: hard-sphere mean free path $\\lambda=\\dfrac{kT}{4\\pi\\sqrt2\\,r^2p}$ (from $\\lambda=V/(4\\pi\\sqrt2 r^2N)$ and $pV=NkT$); then $\\lambda=v\\,t_{mean}$.\n2. (a) $\\lambda=\\dfrac{(1.38\\times10^{-23})(300)}{4\\pi\\sqrt2\\,(2.0\\times10^{-10})^2(1.01\\times10^5)}=5.8\\times10^{-8}$ m.\n3. (b) $t_{mean}=\\dfrac{\\lambda}{v}=\\dfrac{5.8\\times10^{-8}}{484}=1.2\\times10^{-10}$ s.\n4. So the molecule undergoes about $10^{10}$ collisions per second.\n5. Check: λ is several hundred times r, and it is independent of molecular speed, while the mean free time depends on speed.","answers":["(a) λ ≈ 5.8×10⁻⁸ m","(b) t_mean ≈ 1.2×10⁻¹⁰ s (about 10¹⁰ collisions per second)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 18.8"},{"id":"19.1","title":"Work done in an isothermal ideal-gas expansion","q":"As an ideal gas undergoes an isothermal (constant-temperature) expansion at temperature T, its volume changes from V₁ to V₂. How much work does the gas do? Give the result in terms of n, R, T and the volumes, and also in terms of the initial and final pressures.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: the pressure varies during the process, so use $W=\\int_{V_1}^{V_2}p\\,dV$, not $p\\Delta V$.\n2. Equation of state: $p=nRT/V$ with T constant.\n3. $W=\\int_{V_1}^{V_2}\\dfrac{nRT}{V}dV=nRT\\int_{V_1}^{V_2}\\dfrac{dV}{V}=nRT\\ln\\dfrac{V_2}{V_1}$.\n4. Since $p_1V_1=p_2V_2$ at constant T, $V_2/V_1=p_1/p_2$, so $W=nRT\\ln(p_1/p_2)$.\n5. Check: in an expansion $V_2\u003eV_1$ the logarithm is positive, so W \u003e 0. For an isothermal compression $V_2\u003cV_1$, W is negative. For an ideal gas ΔU = 0, so Q = W.","answers":["W = nRT ln(V₂/V₁)","W = nRT ln(p₁/p₂); positive for an expansion"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 19.1"},{"id":"19.2","title":"Climbing stairs to burn off a dessert","q":"You propose to climb several flights of stairs to work off the energy you took in by eating a 900-calorie hot fudge sundae. How high must you climb? Assume that your mass is 60.0 kg and that 1 food calorie = 1 kcal = 4186 J.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: the system is your body; to end in the same state (no fatter, no leaner) there is no net change in internal energy, ΔU = 0.\n2. First law: $0=Q-W$, so $W=Q$. Eating the sundae is a heat input $Q=900\\ \\mathrm{kcal}=(900)(4186)=3.77\\times10^6$ J.\n3. Work done climbing a height h: $W=mgh$.\n4. $h=\\dfrac{Q}{mg}=\\dfrac{3.77\\times10^6}{(60.0)(9.80)}=6410$ m.\n5. Check: this assumes 100% efficiency in converting food energy to mechanical work; real muscles are far less efficient, so you would need to climb much higher (unrealistic).","answers":["h ≈ 6410 m (assuming 100% efficiency)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 19.2"},{"id":"19.4","title":"Comparing heat, work and ΔU along different paths","q":"The pV-diagram shows a series of thermodynamic processes among states a, b, c and d. In process ab, 150 J of heat is added to the system; in process bd, 600 J of heat is added. Find (a) the internal energy change in process ab; (b) the internal energy change in process abd (a→b→d); and (c) the total heat added in process acd (a→c→d, with process ac at constant pressure and cd at constant volume).","figs":["figures/ex-phys/ex19-4.webp"],"figDesc":"pV-diagram: a at p = 3.0×10⁴ Pa, V = 2.0×10⁻³ m³; b directly above a at p = 8.0×10⁴ Pa and the same volume; c at the right of a at the same pressure (3.0×10⁴ Pa) and V = 5.0×10⁻³ m³; d at p = 8.0×10⁴ Pa, V = 5.0×10⁻³ m³. Process ab is vertical (constant volume), bd is horizontal (constant pressure), ac is horizontal and cd is vertical.","sol":"1. Approach: first law ΔU = Q − W on each step; ΔU is path independent.\n2. (a) ab is at constant volume so $W_{ab}=0$ and $\\Delta U_{ab}=Q_{ab}=150$ J.\n3. (b) bd is at constant pressure 8.0×10⁴ Pa: $W_{bd}=p(V_2-V_1)=(8.0\\times10^4)(5.0\\times10^{-3}-2.0\\times10^{-3})=240$ J. For abd: $W=0+240=240$ J, $Q=150+600=750$ J, so $\\Delta U_{abd}=750-240=510$ J.\n4. (c) ΔU is the same for acd: $\\Delta U_{acd}=510$ J. Work: $W_{ac}=p(V_2-V_1)=(3.0\\times10^4)(3.0\\times10^{-3})=90$ J and $W_{cd}=0$, so $W_{acd}=90$ J.\n5. $Q_{acd}=\\Delta U_{acd}+W_{acd}=510+90=600$ J.\n6. Check: ΔU is the same (510 J) for abd and acd, but W (240 vs 90 J) and Q (750 vs 600 J) differ because they depend on the path.","answers":["(a) ΔU_ab = 150 J","(b) ΔU_abd = 510 J","(c) Q_acd = 600 J"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 19.4"},{"id":"19.5","title":"Boiling one gram of water at atmospheric pressure","q":"One gram of water (1 cm³) becomes 1671 cm³ of steam when boiled at a constant pressure of 1 atm (1.013×10⁵ Pa). The heat of vaporization at this pressure is L_v = 2.256×10⁶ J/kg. Compute (a) the work done by the water when it vaporizes and (b) its increase in internal energy.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: constant pressure, so $W=p(V_2-V_1)$; heat from $Q=mL_v$; then the first law gives ΔU.\n2. (a) $W=(1.013\\times10^5\\ \\mathrm{Pa})(1671\\times10^{-6}-1\\times10^{-6})\\ \\mathrm{m^3}=169$ J.\n3. (b) $Q=mL_v=(10^{-3}\\ \\mathrm{kg})(2.256\\times10^6)=2256$ J.\n4. $\\Delta U=Q-W=2256-169=2087$ J.\n5. Check: most of the added heat stays in the system as internal energy (potential energy of pulling molecules apart); only about 7% leaves as expansion work.","answers":["(a) W ≈ 169 J","(b) ΔU ≈ 2087 J (Q = 2256 J)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 19.5"},{"id":"19.6","title":"Internal energy change when cooling a room of air","q":"A typical dorm room or bedroom contains about 2500 moles of air. Find the change in the internal energy of this much air when it is cooled from 35.0°C to 26.0°C at a constant pressure of 1.00 atm. Treat the air as an ideal gas with γ = 1.400 (R = 8.314 J/mol·K).","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: for an ideal gas ΔU = nC_VΔT in any process, even though the pressure (not volume) is constant here.\n2. Find C_V: $C_p=C_V+R$ and $\\gamma=C_p/C_V$ give $C_V=\\dfrac{R}{\\gamma-1}=\\dfrac{8.314}{0.400}=20.79$ J/(mol·K).\n3. $\\Delta T=26.0-35.0=-9.0$ K.\n4. $\\Delta U=nC_V\\Delta T=(2500)(20.79)(-9.0)=-4.7\\times10^5$ J.\n5. Check: ΔU is negative because the gas is cooled; an air conditioner must remove at least this much energy from the room air.","answers":["ΔU ≈ −4.7×10⁵ J (C_V = 20.79 J/mol·K)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 19.6"},{"id":"19.7","title":"Adiabatic compression of air in a diesel engine","q":"The compression ratio of a diesel engine is 15.0 to 1; that is, air in a cylinder is compressed to 1/15.0 of its initial volume. (a) If the initial pressure is 1.01×10⁵ Pa and the initial temperature is 27°C (300 K), find the final pressure and the temperature after adiabatic compression. (b) How much work does the gas do during the compression if the initial volume of the cylinder is 1.00 L = 1.00×10⁻³ m³? Use C_V = 20.8 J/mol·K and γ = 1.400 for air.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: adiabatic ideal gas: $T_1V_1^{\\gamma-1}=T_2V_2^{\\gamma-1}$ and $p_1V_1^{\\gamma}=p_2V_2^{\\gamma}$.\n2. (a) $T_2=T_1(V_1/V_2)^{\\gamma-1}=(300\\ \\mathrm{K})(15.0)^{0.40}=886$ K $=613$°C.\n3. $p_2=p_1(V_1/V_2)^{\\gamma}=(1.01\\times10^5)(15.0)^{1.40}=44.8\\times10^5$ Pa ≈ 44 atm.\n4. (b) $W=\\dfrac{1}{\\gamma-1}(p_1V_1-p_2V_2)$ with $V_2=V_1/15.0$: $W=\\dfrac{1}{0.400}\\left[(1.01\\times10^5)(1.00\\times10^{-3})-(44.8\\times10^5)\\dfrac{1.00\\times10^{-3}}{15.0}\\right]=-494$ J.\n5. Check with $W=nC_V(T_1-T_2)$: $n=p_1V_1/RT_1=0.0405$ mol, so $W=(0.0405)(20.8)(300-886)=-494$ J. W is negative because the gas is compressed.\n6. Isothermal compression would give only 15.0 atm; the high final temperature ignites injected fuel without a spark.","answers":["T₂ ≈ 886 K (613°C)","p₂ ≈ 44.8×10⁵ Pa ≈ 44 atm","W ≈ −494 J"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 19.7"},{"id":"20.1","title":"Efficiency, power and fuel use of a truck engine","q":"A gasoline truck engine takes in 10,000 J of heat and delivers 2000 J of mechanical work per cycle. The heat is obtained by burning gasoline with heat of combustion L_c = 5.0×10⁴ J/g. (a) What is the thermal efficiency of this engine? (b) How much heat is discarded in each cycle? (c) If the engine goes through 25 cycles per second, what is its power output in watts? In horsepower (1 hp = 746 W)? (d) How much gasoline is burned in each cycle? (e) How much gasoline is burned per second?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: energy-flow diagram per cycle with $Q_H=10{,}000$ J in and $W=2000$ J out; use $e=W/Q_H$ and $W=Q_H+Q_C$.\n2. (a) $e=\\dfrac{W}{Q_H}=\\dfrac{2000}{10{,}000}=0.20=20\\%$.\n3. (b) $Q_C=W-Q_H=2000-10{,}000=-8000$ J, so 8000 J is discarded each cycle.\n4. (c) $P=(2000\\ \\mathrm{J/cycle})(25\\ \\mathrm{cycles/s})=50{,}000$ W $=50$ kW $=67$ hp.\n5. (d) $m=\\dfrac{Q_H}{L_c}=\\dfrac{10{,}000}{5.0\\times10^4\\ \\mathrm{J/g}}=0.20$ g per cycle.\n6. (e) $(0.20\\ \\mathrm{g/cycle})(25\\ \\mathrm{cycles/s})=5.0$ g/s.\n7. Check: 20% is typical for cars and trucks if W counts only the work delivered to the wheels.","answers":["(a) e = 0.20","(b) 8000 J discarded per cycle","(c) 50 kW ≈ 67 hp","(d) 0.20 g per cycle","(e) 5.0 g/s"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 20.1"},{"id":"20.3","title":"Complete analysis of a Carnot cycle for a diatomic gas","q":"Suppose 0.200 mol of an ideal diatomic gas (γ = 1.40, C_V = 20.8 J/mol·K) undergoes a Carnot cycle between 227°C and 27°C, starting at p_a = 10.0×10⁵ Pa at point a (the hot isotherm). The volume doubles during the isothermal expansion a→b; then adiabatic expansion b→c, isothermal compression c→d, and adiabatic compression d→a. (a) Find the pressure and volume at points a, b, c and d. (b) Find Q, W and ΔU for each step and for the entire cycle. (c) Find the efficiency directly from the results of part (b) and compare it with the value from the Carnot formula.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Temperatures: T_H = 500 K, T_C = 300 K.\n2. (a) $V_a=\\dfrac{nRT_H}{p_a}=\\dfrac{(0.200)(8.314)(500)}{10.0\\times10^5}=8.31\\times10^{-4}$ m³; $V_b=2V_a=16.6\\times10^{-4}$ m³; $p_b=p_aV_a/V_b=5.00\\times10^5$ Pa.\n3. Adiabats: $V_c=V_b(T_H/T_C)^{1/(\\gamma-1)}=(16.6\\times10^{-4})(500/300)^{2.5}=59.6\\times10^{-4}$ m³, $p_c=nRT_C/V_c=0.837\\times10^5$ Pa; $V_d=V_a(T_H/T_C)^{2.5}=29.8\\times10^{-4}$ m³, $p_d=1.67\\times10^5$ Pa.\n4. (b) a→b: $\\Delta U=0$, $Q=W=nRT_H\\ln2=576$ J. b→c: $Q=0$, $W=-\\Delta U=nC_V(T_H-T_C)=832$ J, $\\Delta U=-832$ J. c→d: $\\Delta U=0$, $Q=W=nRT_C\\ln(V_d/V_c)=-346$ J. d→a: $Q=0$, $W=-832$ J, $\\Delta U=+832$ J.\n5. Totals: Q = 230 J, W = 230 J, ΔU = 0 (adiabatic works cancel).\n6. (c) $e=W/Q_H=230/576=0.40$; Carnot: $1-T_C/T_H=1-300/500=0.40$. Agreement.","answers":["(a) a: 10.0×10⁵ Pa, 8.31×10⁻⁴ m³; b: 5.00×10⁵ Pa, 16.6×10⁻⁴ m³; c: 0.837×10⁵ Pa, 59.6×10⁻⁴ m³; d: 1.67×10⁵ Pa, 29.8×10⁻⁴ m³","(b) Q: 576, 0, −346, 0 J; W: 576, 832, −346, −832 J; ΔU: 0, −832, 0, +832 J; totals Q = W = 230 J, ΔU = 0","(c) e = 230/576 = 0.40 = 1 − T_C/T_H"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 20.3"},{"id":"20.6","title":"Entropy change when heating water","q":"One kilogram of water at 0°C is heated to 100°C. Compute its change in entropy. Assume that the specific heat of water is constant at 4190 J/kg·K over this temperature range.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: the entropy change depends only on the end states, so use a reversible process: heat the water through infinitesimal steps with $dQ=mc\\,dT$.\n2. $\\Delta S=\\int_1^2\\dfrac{dQ}{T}=\\int_{T_1}^{T_2}\\dfrac{mc\\,dT}{T}=mc\\ln\\dfrac{T_2}{T_1}$.\n3. $T_1=273$ K, $T_2=373$ K: $\\Delta S=(1.00)(4190)\\ln\\dfrac{373}{273}=1.31\\times10^3$ J/K.\n4. Check: ΔS is positive since the system absorbs heat. Do not use $Q/T$ here: that applies only when T is constant (isothermal).","answers":["ΔS ≈ 1.31×10³ J/K (positive)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 20.6"},{"id":"20.8","title":"Entropy change in a free expansion","q":"A partition divides a thermally insulated box into two compartments, each of volume V. Initially, one compartment contains n moles of an ideal gas at temperature T, and the other compartment is evacuated. We break the partition and the gas expands, filling both compartments. What is the entropy change in this process? Evaluate it for n = 1 mol (R = 8.314 J/mol·K).","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: no heat flows (Q = 0) and no work is done (W = 0), so ΔU = 0, and for an ideal gas T is unchanged. The process is irreversible (no equilibrium states), so $\\Delta S=\\int dQ/T$ cannot be used along the actual path.\n2. ΔS depends only on end states, so pick a reversible path with the same end points: isothermal expansion from V to 2V at temperature T.\n3. For that path ΔU = 0, so $Q=W=nRT\\ln(2V/V)=nRT\\ln2$.\n4. $\\Delta S=\\dfrac{Q}{T}=nR\\ln2$.\n5. For 1 mol: $\\Delta S=(1)(8.314)(\\ln2)=5.76$ J/K.\n6. Check: positive, as expected since the molecules have more room (more disorder). For expansion V → xV: ΔS = nR ln x.","answers":["ΔS = nR ln 2","For 1 mol: ΔS ≈ 5.76 J/K"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 20.8"},{"id":"20.9","title":"Entropy bookkeeping for a Carnot engine","q":"A Carnot engine takes 2000 J of heat from a reservoir at 500 K, does 600 J of work, and discards 1400 J of heat to a reservoir at 350 K. What is the total entropy change of the engine during one cycle? What is the total entropy change of its environment (the two reservoirs) during the cycle?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: all four steps are reversible; adiabatic steps contribute zero entropy change, so only the two isothermal steps count: $\\Delta S=Q/T$.\n2. Isothermal expansion at T_H = 500 K: $\\Delta S_H=\\dfrac{Q_H}{T_H}=\\dfrac{2000}{500}=+4.0$ J/K.\n3. Isothermal compression at T_C = 350 K: $\\Delta S_C=\\dfrac{Q_C}{T_C}=\\dfrac{-1400}{350}=-4.0$ J/K.\n4. Engine: $\\Delta S_{tot}=4.0+(-4.0)=0$, as it must be for a state function over a cycle.\n5. Environment: the hot reservoir loses 2000 J: $-2000/500=-4.0$ J/K; the cold reservoir gains 1400 J: $+1400/350=+4.0$ J/K; total 0.\n6. Conclusion: for a reversible cycle the total entropy change of system plus environment is zero; irreversible cycles (Otto, Diesel) would give a positive total.","answers":["Engine: ΔS_tot = 0 (+4.0 J/K at T_H, −4.0 J/K at T_C)","Environment: −4.0 + 4.0 = 0 J/K"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 20.9"},{"id":"20.10","title":"Entropy produced by mixing hot and cold water","q":"Suppose 1.00 kg of water at 100°C is placed in thermal contact with 1.00 kg of water at 0°C. What is the total change in entropy? Assume that the specific heat of water is constant at 4190 J/kg·K over this temperature range.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: the heat flow is irreversible, but ΔS depends only on end states; equal masses give a final temperature of 50°C = 323 K.\n2. Compute each entropy change along a reversible path (temperature not constant): $\\Delta S=mc\\ln(T_2/T_1)$.\n3. Hot water (373 K → 323 K): $\\Delta S_H=(1.00)(4190)\\ln\\dfrac{323}{373}=-603$ J/K.\n4. Cold water (273 K → 323 K): $\\Delta S_C=(1.00)(4190)\\ln\\dfrac{323}{273}=+705$ J/K.\n5. Total: $\\Delta S_{tot}=-603+705=+102$ J/K.\n6. Check: the increase exceeds the decrease for any irreversible process; the lost opportunity is the chance to run an engine between the hot and cold water.","answers":["ΔS_hot ≈ −603 J/K, ΔS_cold ≈ +705 J/K","Total ΔS ≈ +102 J/K (positive)"],"src":"Young \u0026 Freedman Example 20.10"}];
